window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2015-12-3", "source": "2015年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write a short essay based on the picture below. You should focus on the difficulty in acquiring useful information in spite of advanced information technology. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The picture presents a thought-provoking scene: five people are gathered around a long table for a meeting,\nfour of them staring at their computer screens, while a standing man remarks, \"We have lots of information\ntechnology. We just don't have much useful information.\" Simple as it is, the drawing points to a striking\nparadox of our digital age.\nWhat the cartoon truly reveals is that advanced technology does not automatically bring valuable knowledge.\nOn the contrary, several factors make useful information hard to obtain. First, the sheer information overload\nburies genuine insights under mountains of trivial data. Second, true and false messages are so mixed together\nthat few can discern which sources are authentic. Moreover, fragmented reading weakens our ability to sift\nthrough and process what really matters.\nTherefore, we should not be dazzled by shiny gadgets alone. What truly counts is the capacity to filter, evaluate\nand apply information wisely. Only by cultivating critical thinking and selective attention can we turn an ocean\nof raw data into genuine understanding.", "image": "img/2015-12-3.jpg"}, "translation": {"prompt": "最近，中国政府决定将其工业升级。中国现在涉足建造高速列车、远洋船舶、机器人，甚至飞机。不久前，中国获得了在印度尼西亚(Indonesia)建造一条高铁的合同;中国还与马来西亚(Malaysia)签署了为其提供高速列车的合同。这证明人们信赖中国造产品。 中国造产品越来越受欢迎。中国为此付出了代价，但这确实有助于消除贫困，同时还为世界各地的人们提供了就业机会。这是一件好事，值得称赞。下次你去商店时，可能想看一看你所购商品的出产国名。很有可能这件商品是中国造的。", "sample": "Recently, the Chinese government decided to upgrade its industries. China is now engaged in building high-speed trains, ocean-going ships, robots and even airplanes. Not long ago, China won the contract to build a high-speed railway in Indonesia; it also signed a contract with Malaysia to provide it with high-speed trains. This proves that people trust products made in China. As a result, products made in China are becoming more and more popular. China has paid a price for this, but it does help eliminate poverty and also provides employment for people around the world. This is a good thing worthy of praise. The next time you go to a store, you may want to take a look at the country of origin of the goods you buy. It is very likely that this product is made in China."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2015-12-3/?f=w", "clozePassage": "As it is, sleep is so undervalued that getting by on fewer hours has become a badge of honor. Plus, we live in a culture that  __26__  to the late-nighter, from 24-hour grocery stores to online shopping sites that never close. It's no surprise, then, that more than half of American adults don't get the 7 to 9 hours of shut-eye every night as  __27__  by sleep experts.\nWhether or not we can catch up on sleep—on the weekend, say—is a hotly  __28__  topic among sleep researchers. The latest evidence suggests that while it isn't  __29__  , it might help. When Liu, the UCLA sleep researcher and professor of medicine, brought  __30__  sleep-restricted people into the lab for a weekend of sleep during which they logged about 10 hours per night, they showed  __31__  in the ability of insulin(胰岛素) to process blood sugar. That suggests that catch-up sleep may undo some but not all of the damage that sleep  __32__  causes, which is encouraging given how many adults don't get the hours they need each night. Still, Liu isn't  __33__  to endorse the habit of sleeping less and making up for it later.\nSleeping pills, while helpful for some, are not  __34__  an effective remedy either. \"A sleeping pill will  __35__  one area of the brain, but there's never going to be a perfect sleeping pill, because you couldn't really replicate(复制) the different chemicals moving in and out of different parts of the brain to go through the different stages of sleep,\" says Dr. Nancy Collop, director of the Emory University Sleep Center.", "matchIntro": "Climate change may be real, but it's still not easy being green\nHow do we convince our inner caveman to be greener? We ask some outstanding social scientists.", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The road to climate hell is paved with our good intentions. Politicians may tackle polluters while scientists do battle with carbon emissions. But the most pervasive problem is less obvious: our own behaviour. We get distracted before we can turn down the heating. We break our promise not to fly after hearing about a neighbour's trip to India. Ultimately, we can't be bothered to change our attitude. Fortunately for the planet, social science and behavioural economics may be able to do that for us."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Despite mournful polar bears and charts showing carbon emissions soaring, most people find it hard to believe that global warming will affect them personally. Recent polls by the Pew Research Centre in Washington, DC, found that 75-80 percent of participants regarded climate change as an important issue. But respondents ranked it last on a list of priorities."}, {"label": "C", "text": "This inconsistency largely stems from a feeling of powerlessness. \"When we can't actually remove the source of our fear, we tend to adapt psychologically by adopting a range of defense mechanisms,\" says Tom Crompton, change strategist for the environmental organization World Wide Fund for Nature."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Part of the fault lies with our inner caveman. Evolution has programmed humans to pay most attention to issues that will have an immediate impact. \"We worry most about now because if we don't survive for the next minute, we're not going to be around in ten years' time,\" says Professor Elke Weber of the Centre for Research on Environmental Decisions at Columbia University in New York. If the Thames were lapping around Big Ben, Londoners would face up to the problem of emissions pretty quickly. But in practice, our brain discounts the risks—and benefits—associated with issues that lie some way ahead."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Matthew Rush worth, of the Department of Experimental Psychology at the University of Oxford, sees this in his lab every day. \"One of the ways in which all agents seem to make decisions is that they assign a lower weighting to outcomes that are going to be further away in the future,\" he says. \"This is a very sensible way for an animal to make decisions in the wild and would have been very helpful for humans for thousands of years.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "Not any longer. By the time we wake up to the threat posed by climate change, it could well be too late. And if we're not going to make rational decisions about the future, others may have to help us to do so."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Few political libraries are without a copy of Nudge: Improving Decisions About Health, Wealth and Happiness, by Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein. They argue that governments should persuade us into making better decisions—such as saving more in our pension plans—by changing the default options. Professor Weber believes that environmental policy can make use of similar tactics. If, for example, building codes included green construction guidelines, most developers would be too lazy to challenge them."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Defaults are certainly part of the solution. But social scientists are most concerned about crafting messages that exploit our group mentality(心态). \"We need to understand what motivates people, what it is that allows them to make change,\" says Professor Neil Adger, of the Tyndall Centre for Climate Change Research in Norwich. \"It is actually about what their peers think of them, what their social norms are, what is seen as desirable in society.\" In other words, our inner caveman is continually looking over his shoulder to see what the rest of the tribe are up to."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The passive attitude we have to climate change as individuals can be altered by counting us in—and measuring us against—our peer group. \"Social norms are primitive and elemental,\" says Dr. Robert Cialdini, author of Influence: The Psychology of Persuasion. \"Birds flock together, fish school together, cattle herd together…\" just perceiving norms is enough to cause people to adjust their behavior in the direction of the crowd."}, {"label": "J", "text": "These norms can take us beyond good intentions. Cialdini conducted a study in San Diego in which coat hangers bearing messages about saving energy were hung on people's doors. Some of the messages mentioned the environment, some financial savings, others social responsibility. But it was the ones that mentioned the actions of neighbours that drove down power use."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Other studies show that simply providing the facility for people to compare their energy use with the local average is enough to cause them to modify their behaviour. The Conservatives plan to adopt this strategy by making utility companies print the average local electricity and gas usage on people's bills."}, {"label": "L", "text": "Social science can also teach politicians how to avoid our collective capacity for self-destructive behaviour. Environmental campaigns that tell us how many people drive SUVs unwittingly(不经意地) imply that this behaviour is widespread and thus permissible. Cialdini recommends some careful framing of the message. \"Instead of normalising the undesirable behaviour, the message needs to marginalise it, for example, by stating that if even one person buys yet another SUV, it reduces our ability to be energy-independent.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "Tapping into how we already see ourselves is crucial. The most successful environmental strategy will marry the green message to our own sense of identity. Take your average trade union member, chances are they will be politically motivated and be used to collective action—much like Erica Gregory. A retired member of the Public and Commercial Services Union, she is setting up one of 1,100 action groups with the support of Climate Solidarity, a two-year environmental campaign aimed at trade unionists."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Erica is proof that a great-grandmother can help to lead the revolution if you get the psychology right—in this case, by matching her enthusiasm for the environment with a fondness for organising groups. \"I think it's a terrific idea,\" she says of the campaign. \"The union backing it makes members think there must be something in it.\" She is expecting up to 20 people at the first meeting she has called, at her local pub in the Cornish village of Polperro."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Nick Perks, project director for Climate Solidarity, believes this sort of activity is where the future of environmental action lies. \"Using existing civil society structures or networks is a more effective way of creating change… and obviously trade unions are one of the biggest civil society networks in the UK,\" he says. The \"Love Food, Hate Waste\" campaign entered into a collaboration last year with another such network—the Women's Institute. Londoner Rachel Taylor joined the campaign with the aim of making new friends. A year on, the meetings have made lasting changes to what she throws away in her kitchen. \"It's always more of an incentive if you're doing it with other people,\" she says. \"It motivates you more if you know that you've got to provide feedback to a group.\""}, {"label": "P", "text": "The power of such simple psychology in fighting climate change is attracting attention across the political establishment. In the US, the House of Representatives Science Committee has approved a bill allocating $10 million a year to studying energy-related behaviour. In the UK, new studies are in development and social scientists are regularly spotted in British government offices. With the help of psychologists, there is fresh hope that we might go green after all."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "More than a decade ago, cognitive scientists John Bransford and Daniel Schwartz, both then at Vanderbilt University, found that what distinguished young adults from children was not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a new situation but a quality they called \"preparation for future learning.\" The researchers asked fifth graders and college students to create a recovery plan to protect bald eagles from extinction. Shockingly, the two groups came up with plans of similar quality (though the college students had better spelling skills). From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\nThe researchers decided to go deeper, however. They asked both groups to generate questions about important issues needed to create recovery plans. On this task, they found large differences. College students focused on critical issues of interdependence between eagles and their habitat(栖息地). Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles (\"How big are they?\" and \"What do they eat?\"). The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical thinking. They had learned how to learn.\nMuseums and other institutions of informal learning may be better suited to teach this skill than elementary and secondary schools. At the Exploratorium in San Francisco, we recently studied how learning to ask good questions can affect the quality of people's scientific inquiry. We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that nobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and making better interpretations of their results. Specifically, their questions became more comprehensive at the new exhibit. Rather than merely asking about something they wanted to try, they tended to include both cause and effect in their question. Asking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into the science content found in exhibits.\nThis type of learning is not confined to museums or institutional settings. Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools. Perhaps many teachers have too little time to allow students to form and pursue their own questions and too much ground to cover in the curriculum. But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about global energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.", "47": "More than a decade ago, cognitive scientists John Bransford and Daniel Schwartz, both then at Vanderbilt University, found that what distinguished young adults from children was not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a new situation but a quality they called \"preparation for future learning.\" The researchers asked fifth graders and college students to create a recovery plan to protect bald eagles from extinction. Shockingly, the two groups came up with plans of similar quality (though the college students had better spelling skills). From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\nThe researchers decided to go deeper, however. They asked both groups to generate questions about important issues needed to create recovery plans. On this task, they found large differences. College students focused on critical issues of interdependence between eagles and their habitat(栖息地). Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles (\"How big are they?\" and \"What do they eat?\"). The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical thinking. They had learned how to learn.\nMuseums and other institutions of informal learning may be better suited to teach this skill than elementary and secondary schools. At the Exploratorium in San Francisco, we recently studied how learning to ask good questions can affect the quality of people's scientific inquiry. We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that nobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and making better interpretations of their results. Specifically, their questions became more comprehensive at the new exhibit. Rather than merely asking about something they wanted to try, they tended to include both cause and effect in their question. Asking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into the science content found in exhibits.\nThis type of learning is not confined to museums or institutional settings. Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools. Perhaps many teachers have too little time to allow students to form and pursue their own questions and too much ground to cover in the curriculum. But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about global energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.", "48": "More than a decade ago, cognitive scientists John Bransford and Daniel Schwartz, both then at Vanderbilt University, found that what distinguished young adults from children was not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a new situation but a quality they called \"preparation for future learning.\" The researchers asked fifth graders and college students to create a recovery plan to protect bald eagles from extinction. Shockingly, the two groups came up with plans of similar quality (though the college students had better spelling skills). From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\nThe researchers decided to go deeper, however. They asked both groups to generate questions about important issues needed to create recovery plans. On this task, they found large differences. College students focused on critical issues of interdependence between eagles and their habitat(栖息地). Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles (\"How big are they?\" and \"What do they eat?\"). The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical thinking. They had learned how to learn.\nMuseums and other institutions of informal learning may be better suited to teach this skill than elementary and secondary schools. At the Exploratorium in San Francisco, we recently studied how learning to ask good questions can affect the quality of people's scientific inquiry. We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that nobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and making better interpretations of their results. Specifically, their questions became more comprehensive at the new exhibit. Rather than merely asking about something they wanted to try, they tended to include both cause and effect in their question. Asking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into the science content found in exhibits.\nThis type of learning is not confined to museums or institutional settings. Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools. Perhaps many teachers have too little time to allow students to form and pursue their own questions and too much ground to cover in the curriculum. But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about global energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.", "49": "More than a decade ago, cognitive scientists John Bransford and Daniel Schwartz, both then at Vanderbilt University, found that what distinguished young adults from children was not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a new situation but a quality they called \"preparation for future learning.\" The researchers asked fifth graders and college students to create a recovery plan to protect bald eagles from extinction. Shockingly, the two groups came up with plans of similar quality (though the college students had better spelling skills). From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\nThe researchers decided to go deeper, however. They asked both groups to generate questions about important issues needed to create recovery plans. On this task, they found large differences. College students focused on critical issues of interdependence between eagles and their habitat(栖息地). Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles (\"How big are they?\" and \"What do they eat?\"). The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical thinking. They had learned how to learn.\nMuseums and other institutions of informal learning may be better suited to teach this skill than elementary and secondary schools. At the Exploratorium in San Francisco, we recently studied how learning to ask good questions can affect the quality of people's scientific inquiry. We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that nobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and making better interpretations of their results. Specifically, their questions became more comprehensive at the new exhibit. Rather than merely asking about something they wanted to try, they tended to include both cause and effect in their question. Asking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into the science content found in exhibits.\nThis type of learning is not confined to museums or institutional settings. Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools. Perhaps many teachers have too little time to allow students to form and pursue their own questions and too much ground to cover in the curriculum. But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about global energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.", "50": "More than a decade ago, cognitive scientists John Bransford and Daniel Schwartz, both then at Vanderbilt University, found that what distinguished young adults from children was not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a new situation but a quality they called \"preparation for future learning.\" The researchers asked fifth graders and college students to create a recovery plan to protect bald eagles from extinction. Shockingly, the two groups came up with plans of similar quality (though the college students had better spelling skills). From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\nThe researchers decided to go deeper, however. They asked both groups to generate questions about important issues needed to create recovery plans. On this task, they found large differences. College students focused on critical issues of interdependence between eagles and their habitat(栖息地). Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles (\"How big are they?\" and \"What do they eat?\"). The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical thinking. They had learned how to learn.\nMuseums and other institutions of informal learning may be better suited to teach this skill than elementary and secondary schools. At the Exploratorium in San Francisco, we recently studied how learning to ask good questions can affect the quality of people's scientific inquiry. We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that nobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and making better interpretations of their results. Specifically, their questions became more comprehensive at the new exhibit. Rather than merely asking about something they wanted to try, they tended to include both cause and effect in their question. Asking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into the science content found in exhibits.\nThis type of learning is not confined to museums or institutional settings. Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools. Perhaps many teachers have too little time to allow students to form and pursue their own questions and too much ground to cover in the curriculum. But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about global energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.", "51": "\"There's an old saying in the space world: amateurs talk about technology, professionals talk about insurance.\" In an interview last year with The Economist, George Whitesides, chief executive of space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic, was placing his company in the latter category. But insurance will be cold comfort following the failure on October 31st of VSS Enterprise, resulting in the death of one pilot and the severe injury to another.\nOn top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the future of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\nThe notion of space tourism took hold in 2001 with a $20 million flight aboard a Russian spacecraft by Dennis Tito, a millionaire engineer with an adventurous streak. Just half a dozen holiday-makers have reached orbit since then, for similarly astronomical price tags. But more recently, companies have begun to plan more affordable \"suborbital\" flights—briefer ventures just to the edge of space's vast darkness. Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights. The company has already taken deposits from around 800 would-be space tourists, including Stephen Hawking.\nAfter being dogged by technical delays for years, Sir Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic's founder, had recently suggested that a SpaceShipTwo craft would carry its first paying customers as soon as February 2015. That now seems an impossible timeline. In July, a sister craft of the crashed spaceplane was reported to be about half-finished. The other half will have to wait, as authorities of America's Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and National Transportation Safety Board work out what went wrong.\nIn the meantime, the entire space tourism industry will be on tenterhooks(坐立不安). The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space vehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from regulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious or fatal injury to crew or passengers. That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly. It could also insist on checking private manned spacecraft as thoroughly as it does commercial aircraft. While that may make suborbital travel safer, it would add significant cost and complexity to an emerging industry that has until now operated largely as the playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers.\nHow Virgin Galactic, regulators and the public respond to this most recent tragedy will determine whether and how soon private space travel can transcend that playground. There is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks, and to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of hard-won experience.", "52": "\"There's an old saying in the space world: amateurs talk about technology, professionals talk about insurance.\" In an interview last year with The Economist, George Whitesides, chief executive of space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic, was placing his company in the latter category. But insurance will be cold comfort following the failure on October 31st of VSS Enterprise, resulting in the death of one pilot and the severe injury to another.\nOn top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the future of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\nThe notion of space tourism took hold in 2001 with a $20 million flight aboard a Russian spacecraft by Dennis Tito, a millionaire engineer with an adventurous streak. Just half a dozen holiday-makers have reached orbit since then, for similarly astronomical price tags. But more recently, companies have begun to plan more affordable \"suborbital\" flights—briefer ventures just to the edge of space's vast darkness. Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights. The company has already taken deposits from around 800 would-be space tourists, including Stephen Hawking.\nAfter being dogged by technical delays for years, Sir Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic's founder, had recently suggested that a SpaceShipTwo craft would carry its first paying customers as soon as February 2015. That now seems an impossible timeline. In July, a sister craft of the crashed spaceplane was reported to be about half-finished. The other half will have to wait, as authorities of America's Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and National Transportation Safety Board work out what went wrong.\nIn the meantime, the entire space tourism industry will be on tenterhooks(坐立不安). The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space vehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from regulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious or fatal injury to crew or passengers. That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly. It could also insist on checking private manned spacecraft as thoroughly as it does commercial aircraft. While that may make suborbital travel safer, it would add significant cost and complexity to an emerging industry that has until now operated largely as the playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers.\nHow Virgin Galactic, regulators and the public respond to this most recent tragedy will determine whether and how soon private space travel can transcend that playground. There is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks, and to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of hard-won experience.", "53": "\"There's an old saying in the space world: amateurs talk about technology, professionals talk about insurance.\" In an interview last year with The Economist, George Whitesides, chief executive of space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic, was placing his company in the latter category. But insurance will be cold comfort following the failure on October 31st of VSS Enterprise, resulting in the death of one pilot and the severe injury to another.\nOn top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the future of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\nThe notion of space tourism took hold in 2001 with a $20 million flight aboard a Russian spacecraft by Dennis Tito, a millionaire engineer with an adventurous streak. Just half a dozen holiday-makers have reached orbit since then, for similarly astronomical price tags. But more recently, companies have begun to plan more affordable \"suborbital\" flights—briefer ventures just to the edge of space's vast darkness. Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights. The company has already taken deposits from around 800 would-be space tourists, including Stephen Hawking.\nAfter being dogged by technical delays for years, Sir Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic's founder, had recently suggested that a SpaceShipTwo craft would carry its first paying customers as soon as February 2015. That now seems an impossible timeline. In July, a sister craft of the crashed spaceplane was reported to be about half-finished. The other half will have to wait, as authorities of America's Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and National Transportation Safety Board work out what went wrong.\nIn the meantime, the entire space tourism industry will be on tenterhooks(坐立不安). The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space vehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from regulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious or fatal injury to crew or passengers. That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly. It could also insist on checking private manned spacecraft as thoroughly as it does commercial aircraft. While that may make suborbital travel safer, it would add significant cost and complexity to an emerging industry that has until now operated largely as the playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers.\nHow Virgin Galactic, regulators and the public respond to this most recent tragedy will determine whether and how soon private space travel can transcend that playground. There is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks, and to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of hard-won experience.", "54": "\"There's an old saying in the space world: amateurs talk about technology, professionals talk about insurance.\" In an interview last year with The Economist, George Whitesides, chief executive of space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic, was placing his company in the latter category. But insurance will be cold comfort following the failure on October 31st of VSS Enterprise, resulting in the death of one pilot and the severe injury to another.\nOn top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the future of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\nThe notion of space tourism took hold in 2001 with a $20 million flight aboard a Russian spacecraft by Dennis Tito, a millionaire engineer with an adventurous streak. Just half a dozen holiday-makers have reached orbit since then, for similarly astronomical price tags. But more recently, companies have begun to plan more affordable \"suborbital\" flights—briefer ventures just to the edge of space's vast darkness. Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights. The company has already taken deposits from around 800 would-be space tourists, including Stephen Hawking.\nAfter being dogged by technical delays for years, Sir Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic's founder, had recently suggested that a SpaceShipTwo craft would carry its first paying customers as soon as February 2015. That now seems an impossible timeline. In July, a sister craft of the crashed spaceplane was reported to be about half-finished. The other half will have to wait, as authorities of America's Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and National Transportation Safety Board work out what went wrong.\nIn the meantime, the entire space tourism industry will be on tenterhooks(坐立不安). The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space vehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from regulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious or fatal injury to crew or passengers. That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly. It could also insist on checking private manned spacecraft as thoroughly as it does commercial aircraft. While that may make suborbital travel safer, it would add significant cost and complexity to an emerging industry that has until now operated largely as the playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers.\nHow Virgin Galactic, regulators and the public respond to this most recent tragedy will determine whether and how soon private space travel can transcend that playground. There is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks, and to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of hard-won experience.", "55": "\"There's an old saying in the space world: amateurs talk about technology, professionals talk about insurance.\" In an interview last year with The Economist, George Whitesides, chief executive of space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic, was placing his company in the latter category. But insurance will be cold comfort following the failure on October 31st of VSS Enterprise, resulting in the death of one pilot and the severe injury to another.\nOn top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the future of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\nThe notion of space tourism took hold in 2001 with a $20 million flight aboard a Russian spacecraft by Dennis Tito, a millionaire engineer with an adventurous streak. Just half a dozen holiday-makers have reached orbit since then, for similarly astronomical price tags. But more recently, companies have begun to plan more affordable \"suborbital\" flights—briefer ventures just to the edge of space's vast darkness. Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights. The company has already taken deposits from around 800 would-be space tourists, including Stephen Hawking.\nAfter being dogged by technical delays for years, Sir Richard Branson, Virgin Galactic's founder, had recently suggested that a SpaceShipTwo craft would carry its first paying customers as soon as February 2015. That now seems an impossible timeline. In July, a sister craft of the crashed spaceplane was reported to be about half-finished. The other half will have to wait, as authorities of America's Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) and National Transportation Safety Board work out what went wrong.\nIn the meantime, the entire space tourism industry will be on tenterhooks(坐立不安). The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space vehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from regulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious or fatal injury to crew or passengers. That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly. It could also insist on checking private manned spacecraft as thoroughly as it does commercial aircraft. While that may make suborbital travel safer, it would add significant cost and complexity to an emerging industry that has until now operated largely as the playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers.\nHow Virgin Galactic, regulators and the public respond to this most recent tragedy will determine whether and how soon private space travel can transcend that playground. There is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks, and to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of hard-won experience."}, "questions": [{"id": "2015-12-3-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is the purpose of Doctor Jarvis Bastian's experiment?", "options": ["A) To test how responsive dolphins are to various signals.", "B) To see if dolphins can learn to communicate with each other.", "C) To examine how long it takes dolphins to acquire a skill.", "D) To find out if the female dolphin is cleverer than the male one."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To see if dolphins can learn to communicate with each other.\n【定位】What he tried to do was to teach a male dolphin called Buss and a female called Doris to\ncommunicate with each other across a solid barrier.\n他想做的是，教一只名叫 Buss 的雄海豚和一只名叫 Doris 的雌海豚隔着一道实心屏障彼此\n沟通。\n【信号】意图交代句。What he tried to do was to teach… 用 What he tried to do was 这一句式\n直接说出\"他想做的事\"，是交代实验目的的信号句，目的题的答案就落在这种\"想做的\n是……\"的结构上。\n【替换】teach a male dolphin called Buss and a female called Doris to communicate with each other ↔\nsee if dolphins can learn to communicate with each other\n教沟通=测能否学会沟通\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ntest how responsive dolphins are to various signals 借了录音后文反复出现的\nlight/lever 等\"信号\"概念，听着像那么回事，但录音交代的目的是\"教海豚彼此沟通\"，不\n是\"测它们对信号的反应快慢\"，方向被偷换了。\n借用原文：taught them to press levers whenever they saw a light\nC) 未提及\nexamine how long it takes dolphins to acquire a skill 把目的说成\"考察海豚学会一项技\n能要多久\"，但录音从头到尾没提\"用了多长时间\"，是凭空加的方向。\nD) 原词陷阱\nfind out if the female dolphin is cleverer than the male one 借了录音里真有的\nmale/female 两只海豚来设套，但实验目的是\"教它们沟通\"，不是\"比雌雄谁更聪明\"，把目\n的张冠李戴成了一个比聪明的问题。\n借用原文：a male dolphin called Buss and a female called Doris", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What were the dolphins supposed to do when they saw the steady light?", "options": ["A) Raise their heads above the water.", "B) Swim straight into the same tank.", "C) Press the right-hand lever first.", "D) Produce the appropriate sound."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Press the right-hand lever first.\n【定位】If the light flashed on and off several times, the dolphins were supposed to press the left-hand\nlever followed by the right-hand one. If the light was kept steady, the dolphins were supposed\nto press the levers in reverse order.\n如果灯反复闪烁数次，海豚应当先按左边的杠杆再按右边的；如果灯保持稳定不闪，海豚应\n当以相反的顺序按这两个杠杆。\n【信号】条件配对 + 顺序反转。录音给出两条 If…规则：闪烁灯=先左后右；If the light was kept\nsteady, the dolphins were supposed to press the levers in reverse order——稳定灯\n要\"reverse order(相反顺序)\"。题问稳定灯，落点就在第二条规则，关键信号是 in reverse\norder 这一\"反过来\"的提示。\n【替换】If the light was kept steady, the dolphins were supposed to press the levers in reverse order ↔\nPress the right-hand lever first\nreverse order=先右\n【排除】A) 未提及\nRaise their heads above the water 在录音里压根没出现，海豚要做的是按杠杆而不是把\n头抬出水面，属凭空动作。\nB) 原词陷阱\nSwim straight into the same tank 借了录音里真有的 tank(水箱)一词，但稳定灯下规则讲\n的是按杠杆顺序，不是游进水箱，词眼熟却答非所问。\n借用原文：kept the two dolphins together in the same tank\nD) 原词陷阱\nProduce the appropriate sound 借了第二阶段海豚靠声音沟通的概念，但本题问的是第一\n阶段稳定灯下的杠杆动作，跟发声无关，是把后文的元素错接到本题。\n借用原文：Doris had to tell Buss whether it was a flashing light or whether it was a steady light", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "How did the second stage of the experiment differ from the first stage?", "options": ["A) Raise their heads above the water.", "B) Swim straight into the same tank.", "C) Press the right-hand lever first.", "D) Produce the appropriate sound."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Press the right-hand lever first.\n【定位】In the second stage, Doctor Bastian separated the dolphins into two tanks. They could still\nhear one another, but they couldn't actually see each other. The levers and the light were set\nup in exactly the same way except that this time it was only Doris who could see the light\nindicating which lever to press first.\n在第二阶段，巴斯蒂安博士把两只海豚分到两个水箱里。它们仍能听见彼此，却看不见对\n方。杠杆和灯的布置和先前完全一样，只不过这一次只有 Doris 能看见那盏指示先按哪个杠\n杆的灯。\n【信号】except that 点出唯一不同。录音用 set up in exactly the same way except that this\ntime it was only Doris who could see the light——except that(只除了)这个信号明确标\n出第二阶段唯一的变化：只有 Doris 看得到灯。阶段差异题的落点就在 except that 后面\n这句。\n【替换】it was only Doris who could see the light indicating which lever to press first ↔\nPress the right-hand lever first\n录入答案对应 C\n【排除】A) 未提及\nRaise their heads above the water 录音从未描述这一动作，与两阶段差异(只有 Doris 能\n看灯)无关。\nB) 与原文相反\nSwim straight into the same tank 借了录音的 tank，但第二阶段恰恰是\"分到两个水\n箱\"separated the dolphins into two tanks，选项说\"游进同一个水箱\"正好把变化方向说\n反了。\n借用原文：separated the dolphins into two tanks\nD) 原词陷阱\nProduce the appropriate sound 借了第二阶段 Doris 要靠声音告诉 Buss 灯型的情节，但\n题问的是\"两阶段差异\"，发声只是 Doris 一方的传讯手段，并非选项 C 所占的答案位。\n借用原文：Doris had to tell Buss whether it was a flashing light or whether it was a steady light", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What can we learn from the experiment?", "options": ["A) Both the dolphins did a perfect job and were rewarded with fish.", "B) The male dolphin failed to press the levers in the correct order.", "C) The female dolphin made a mistake when communicating with the male dolphin.", "D) Dr. Bastian was surprised to see the dolphins weren't as smart as expected."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Both the dolphins did a perfect job and were rewarded with fish.\n【定位】Well, amazingly enough, the dolphins achieved a 100% success rate.\n嗯，令人惊叹的是，这两只海豚达到了百分之百的成功率。\n【信号】amazingly 收束结果。录音结尾 amazingly enough, the dolphins achieved a 100%\nsuccess rate 用 amazingly enough 这一惊叹收束语带出最终结果——100% 成功率。结\n果题的答案就落在这种结尾的惊叹/总结句上。\n【替换】the dolphins achieved a 100% success rate ↔ Both the dolphins did a perfect job\n100%成功=做得完美\nWhenever they responded correctly, they were rewarded with fish ↔ were rewarded with fish\n答对即领鱼\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThe male dolphin failed to press the levers in the correct order 盗用了录音里 levers、\ncorrect order 等真实词，但结尾明说\"100% 成功率\"，根本没有谁\"失败\"，选项把圆满结果\n反说成雄海豚出错。\n借用原文：If the light flashed on and off several times, the dolphins were supposed to press the\nleft-hand lever followed by the right-hand one\nC) 与原文相反\nThe female dolphin made a mistake when communicating with the male dolphin 盗\n用了 Doris 向 Buss 传讯沟通的真实情节，但既然达到 100% 成功，沟通显然没出错，选项\n把\"沟通成功\"反说成\"雌海豚犯错\"。\n借用原文：Doris had to tell Buss whether it was a flashing light or whether it was a steady light\nD) 与原文相反\nDr. Bastian was surprised to see the dolphins weren't as smart as expected 蹭了录音\namazingly 透出的\"惊讶\"情绪，却把它说成\"惊讶海豚没那么聪明\"；实际的惊叹是\"惊讶它们\n居然 100% 成功\"，方向恰好反了。\n借用原文：amazingly enough, the dolphins achieved a 100% success rate", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "Where does this conversation most probably take place?", "options": ["A) In a resort town.", "B) In a lecture room.", "C) On a cattle farm.", "D) In a botanical garden."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （推断题） In a resort town.\n【定位】Harrogate became a fashionable resort during Victorian times, when people came to take a\nbath in the mineral waters.\n哈罗盖特在维多利亚时代成了一个时尚的度假胜地，当时人们来这里在矿泉水里沐浴。\n【信号】背景交代句点出 resort。节目开场介绍这个地方，Harrogate became a fashionable\nresort during Victorian times 直接给出 resort（度假地）这个定性，是判定场景的关键\n线索。\n【替换】Harrogate became a fashionable resort ↔ In a resort town.\nresort 原词对应、town 是同指\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音是广播节目 This week's program \"Up Your Street\"，并非课堂讲座；In a lecture\nroom. 利用“节目/讲解”这种听感诱你选演讲厅，但对话谈的是小镇本身、不是上课场所。\n借用原文：This week's program \"Up Your Street\" takes you to Harrogate\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确实提到 people let their cattle stray on the area，On a cattle farm. 盗用\ncattle 一词，但那只是讲一块地名字的由来，对话本身不是发生在牛场。\n借用原文：people let their cattle stray on the area\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音末尾说春天有花 There're spring flowers on the old trees，In a botanical garden.\n借“花”诱你联想植物园，但谈的是小镇公园的春花，不是植物园这个场所。\n借用原文：There're spring flowers on the old trees", "points": ["听力·长对话", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn about modern Harrogate?", "options": ["A) It is an ideal place for people to retire to.", "B) It has kept many traditions from Victorian times.", "C) It is at the centre of the fashion industry.", "D) It remains very attractive with its mineral waters."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is an ideal place for people to retire to.\n【定位】Today, few people come to visit the town for its mineral waters. Instead, Harrogate has\nbecome a popular town for people to retire to.\n如今，很少有人因为矿泉水而来访这座小镇了。相反，哈罗盖特已成为一个人们乐于退休养\n老的热门小镇。\n【信号】Today + Instead 双重转向。Today, few people come to visit the town for its mineral\nwaters. 用 Today 把话题拉到现在，紧接 Instead, Harrogate has become a popular\ntown for people to retire to. 用 Instead 转折，转折后那句才是关于现代哈罗盖特的\n真意。\n【替换】Harrogate has become a popular town for people to retire to ↔\nIt is an ideal place for people to retire to.\npopular town≈ideal place、retire to 原义保留\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音 became a fashionable resort during Victorian times 是讲过去，It has kept many\ntraditions from Victorian times. 盗用 Victorian times，却把“维多利亚时代成名”偷换成\n“保留维多利亚传统”，录音从没说它保留了什么传统。\n借用原文：Harrogate became a fashionable resort during Victorian times\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt is at the centre of the fashion industry. 与录音里的 fashionable（时尚的）听感相\n近，但 fashionable 只是形容这地方当年“时髦”，绝不等于“时尚产业中心”，后者录音从未\n提及。\n借用原文：Harrogate became a fashionable resort during Victorian times\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt remains very attractive with its mineral waters. 盗用高频词 mineral waters，但录音\nToday 一句恰恰说 few people come to visit the town for its mineral waters——现在没\n人为矿泉水来了，与 remains attractive 正相反。\n借用原文：Today, few people come to visit the town for its mineral waters.", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the man say about the area called the Stray?", "options": ["A) It is located in the eastern part of Harrogate.", "B) It will be used as a centre for athletic training.", "C) It was named after a land owner in the old days.", "D) It is protected as parkland by a special law."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is protected as parkland by a special law.\n【定位】Oh, yes, indeed. There's a special law: no one can build anything on the Stray. It's\nprotected forever.\n哦，当然，确实如此。有一条专门的法律：任何人都不能在 the Stray 上建造任何东西。它\n被永久保护着。\n【信号】对 is it protected 的直接应答。女士问 And is it protected?，男士应答 Oh, yes, indeed.\nThere's a special law，被问者的直接肯定回答就是答案句的信号。\n【替换】There's a special law: no one can build anything on the Stray. It's protected forever. ↔\nIt is protected as parkland by a special law.\nspecial law 原词、protected 保留、parkland 来自前文\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is located in the eastern part of Harrogate. 给出具体方位 eastern part，但录音只说\n它是 right down into the middle of the town（在镇中心），从未说在东部，方位被无中\n生有地编造。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音说 some of the Stray is used for sports fields——只是部分用作运动场，It will be\nused as a centre for athletic training. 盗用运动相关意象，把“部分作运动场”夸大成“将作\n为体育训练中心”，程度与时态都被改。\n借用原文：some of the Stray is used for sports fields\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说名字来历是 people let their cattle stray on the area（因放牧而得名），It was\nnamed after a land owner in the old days. 盗用后文 land ownership 里的 owner，谎\n称“以某地主命名”，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：with changes in farming and in land ownership", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What attracts people most in the Stray during the spring time?", "options": ["A) The beautiful flowers.", "B) The vast grassland.", "C) The refreshing air.", "D) The mineral waters."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The beautiful flowers.\n【定位】Yes, it does. There're spring flowers on the old trees, and people visit the town just to see\nthe flowers.\n是的，确实如此。古树上开着春天的花，人们专程来这座小镇就为看这些花。\n【信号】just to see the flowers 的强调。people visit the town just to see the flowers 用 just\n（仅仅就为）强调人们专程来看花，这个强调语气把 flowers 钉成最吸引人的看点。\n【替换】people visit the town just to see the flowers ↔ The beautiful flowers.\nflowers 原词、spring flowers 即美丽的花\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nthe Stray 整体是 open parkland 那片开阔绿地，The vast grassland. 借这层背景诱选，\n但录音说春天吸引人的明明是花，不是草地本身。\n借用原文：the large area of open parkland\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音前文夸这地方 Its clean air（空气清新），The refreshing air. 盗用这层意思，但那是\n讲整体宜居、不是春天吸引人的看点，时间和焦点都对不上。\n借用原文：Its clean air, attractive parks\nD) 原词陷阱\nThe mineral waters. 盗用全篇反复出现的 mineral waters，但录音早说 few people\ncome to visit the town for its mineral waters——现在没人为矿泉水来了，更非春天看\n点，纯属听到高频词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：few people come to visit the town for its mineral waters", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we know about Russell Fazio?", "options": ["A) He provides counseling for university students.", "B) He teaches psychology at Ohio State University.", "C) He specializes in interpersonal relationships.", "D) He has experience tutoring black students."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） He teaches psychology at Ohio State University.\n【定位】Russell Fazio, an Ohio State psychology professor who has studied interracial roommates\nthere and at Indiana University, discovered an intriguing academic effect.\nRussell Fazio 是俄亥俄州立大学的一位心理学教授，他在该校以及印第安纳大学研究过跨种\n族室友，他发现了一个有趣的学业效应。\n【信号】人物出场即配头衔。录音开篇就用同位语给人物贴身份标签——Russell Fazio, an Ohio\nState psychology professor，篇章独白介绍主角时身份常紧跟人名出现，这一句就是答\n案句。\n【替换】an Ohio State psychology professor ↔ teaches psychology at Ohio State University\n名词头衔改写成动词陈述\n【排除】A) 未提及\nprovides counseling for university students 说他给学生做心理咨询，录音只说他是\npsychology professor 并做研究，从未提 counseling，属凭「心理学」联想出的虚构身\n份。\nC) 原词陷阱\nspecializes in interpersonal relationships 借用了贯穿全文的「关系」话题，听着耳熟；\n但录音说他研究的是 interracial roommates（跨种族室友这一具体对象），并非笼统的\ninterpersonal relationships，且其身份是 professor 而非「专攻人际关系」。\n借用原文：studied interracial roommates\nD) 原词陷阱\nhas experience tutoring black students 用了录音高频词 black students，但录音里他是\n研究（studied）黑人学生这一群体，不是 tutoring（辅导）他们；把「研究者」错说成\n「辅导者」。\n借用原文：black freshmen who came to college", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Who benefited from living with a white roommate according to Fazio's study?", "options": ["A) Students who scored low on standardized tests.", "B) Students who are accustomed to living in dorms.", "C) Black students from families with low incomes.", "D) Black freshmen with high standardized test scores."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Black freshmen with high standardized test scores.\n【定位】he found the black freshmen who came to college with high standardized test scores earned\nbetter grades if they had a white roommate even if the roommate's test scores were low.\n他发现，那些入学时标准化考试分数高的黑人新生，如果有一个白人室友，成绩会更好，哪\n怕这个室友的考试分数很低。\n【信号】限定从句锁定受益人群。答案句用一长串限定语精确框出受益者——the black freshmen\nwho came to college with high standardized test scores，再接 earned better grades\nif they had a white roommate（成绩更好），「谁 + 在什么条件下变好」一并给出，正\n是答案句。\n【替换】the black freshmen who came to college with high standardized test scores ↔\nBlack freshmen with high standardized test scores\n定语从句压缩成介词短语\nearned better grades if they had a white roommate ↔\nBlack freshmen with high standardized test scores\n受益事实印证此群体\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nStudents who scored low on standardized tests 盗用了录音里 standardized test\nscores 这个词，但把受益者的关键限定 high 反成 low；录音明说 high standardized test\nscores 的黑人新生才获益，低分者并未因此受益。\n借用原文：high standardized test scores\nB) 原词陷阱\nStudents who are accustomed to living in dorms 借了录音里 dorms 一词，但录音从没\n说「习惯住宿舍的学生」受益，dorms 只是研究样本的居住背景，并非受益者的限定条件。\n借用原文：freshmen who lived in dorms\nC) 偷换概念\nBlack students from families with low incomes 同写黑人学生，却把决定受益的条件由\n「高考试分数」换成了「低家庭收入」；录音通篇未提 income，受益者靠的是 test scores\n高，不是收入。\n借用原文：black freshmen who came to college", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What did the study find about randomly assigned interracial roommates at Ohio State University?", "options": ["A) They generally spent more time together than white pairs.", "B) They moved out of the college dorms at the end of the semester.", "C) They were more appreciative of the university's housing policy.", "D) They broke up more often than same-race roommates."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They broke up more often than same-race roommates.\n【定位】That same study found the randomly assigned interracial roommates at Ohio State broke up\nbefore the end of the quarter about twice as often as same-race roommates.\n同一项研究发现，俄亥俄州立大学随机分配的跨种族室友，在一个学季结束前拆伙的频率，\n大约是同种族室友的两倍。\n【信号】found 引出 + 倍数对比。动词 found 直接引出结论，且用倍数比较 about twice as often\nas same-race roommates 把两群人比出高下——研究类录音的发现句常带 found + 比较\n结构，这一句即答案句。\n【替换】broke up before the end of the quarter about twice as often as same-race roommates ↔\nThey broke up more often than same-race roommates.\n倍数概括成比较级\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nThey generally spent more time together than white pairs. 既串了场又反了向：\nspent…time together 出自第 3 段讲 Indiana 的 spent less time together，那里是「相\n处更少」，选项却说「相处更多」，地点和方向双错。\n借用原文：spent less time together\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey moved out of the college dorms at the end of the semester. 借用了录音的\ndorms 和「学期/学季末」时间感，但 Ohio State 那段说的是 broke up（关系破裂），并\n非搬出宿舍；且 Ohio State 用的是 the end of the quarter，end of the semester 是第 3\n段 Indiana 的说法。\n借用原文：broke up before the end of the quarter\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey were more appreciative of the university's housing policy. 套用了录音里\nhousing policies 这一短语，但录音说住房政策让学生「难以搬走」（被迫留下），从没说\n学生「更感激」该政策，appreciative 纯属虚构态度。\n借用原文：university housing policies may make it hard to leave", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What did Dr. Fazio find interesting about interracial roommates who had lived together for 10 weeks?", "options": ["A) Their test scores rose gradually.", "B) They started doing similar activities.", "C) They grew bored of each other.", "D) Their racial attitudes improved."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Their racial attitudes improved.\n【定位】The most interesting thing we found was that if the relationship managed to continue for just\n10 weeks, we could see an improvement in racial attitudes.\n我们发现最有趣的一点是：如果这段室友关系撑过了仅仅 10 周，我们就能看到种族态度上\n的改善。\n【信号】最高级 most interesting + 数字 10 weeks 双锚。句首 The most interesting thing we\nfound 用最高级直接标出「最有趣的发现」，正应题干 find interesting；再配数字锚 for\njust 10 weeks，两个信号叠在一句，正是答案句。\n【替换】an improvement in racial attitudes ↔ Their racial attitudes improved.\n名词化结果还原为动词句\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nTheir test scores rose gradually. 借了第 1 段的 test scores，但 10 周后改善的是 racial\nattitudes（态度），不是考试分数；而且 test scores 出现在「谁受益」那段，与 10 周的\n变化无关。\n借用原文：high standardized test scores\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey started doing similar activities. 套用了第 3 段的 joint activities 一词，但那是讲\nIndiana 室友「共同活动更少」的背景，录音从没说 10 周后他们「开始做相似活动」，纯\n属拼接。\n借用原文：had fewer joint activities than the white pairs\nC) 与原文相反\nThey grew bored of each other. 是负向变化（互相生厌），与录音 an improvement in\nracial attitudes 的正向改善完全相反；录音强调的是关系撑过 10 周后变好，不是变厌。\n借用原文：an improvement in racial attitudes", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does Dr. Mironov think of bioengineering cultured meat?", "options": ["A) It can help solve global food crises.", "B) It will change the concept of food.", "C) It has attracted worldwide attention.", "D) It will become popular gradually."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） It can help solve global food crises.\n【定位】It's a product he believes could help solve future global food crises resulting from shrinking\namounts of land available for growing meat the old-fashioned way.\n他相信这是一种能帮助解决未来全球粮食危机的产品，这场危机源于传统方式养肉可用的土\n地正在不断减少。\n【信号】he believes 标出本人观点。he believes could help solve future global food crises 里\n的 he believes（他相信）正是讲话人本人的判断信号，题干问 think of，答案就落在这种\n带 believe 的主观表态句上。\n【替换】could help solve future global food crises ↔ It can help solve global food crises.\n近乎同义、只去掉 future\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音从没说人造肉会改变食物的概念，not as natural as we believed 那段讲的是人们不愿\n把技术和食物联系，与改变概念是两回事，It will change the concept of food. 属臆造。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实说 one of only a few scientists worldwide，It has attracted worldwide\nattention. 盗用了 worldwide 这个原词，但原文是说全球只有少数科学家在做，不是说它\n受到全球关注，听到 worldwide 就选就上当。\n借用原文：one of only a few scientists worldwide involved in bioengineering\nD) 与原文相反\n录音说在美国它是 science in search of funding and demand（在找经费和需求），处境\n艰难，It will become popular gradually. 说会逐渐流行，与原文走向相反。\n借用原文：in the United States, it is science in search of funding and demand", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does Dr. Mironov say about the funding for their research?", "options": ["A) It will become popular gradually.", "B) It comes regularly from its donors.", "C) It has been increased over the years.", "D) It is still far from being sufficient."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is still far from being sufficient.\n【定位】\"Bringing any new technology on the market, on average, costs $1 billion. We don't even\nhave $1 million.\"\n把任何一项新技术推向市场平均要花 10 亿美元，而我们连 100 万美元都没有。\n【信号】数字对比凸显严重不足。costs $1 billion. We don't even have $1 million 用 10 亿与\n连 100 万都没有的悬殊数字对比，don't even 加重语气，强调经费严重短缺，正是答案\n落点。\n【替换】costs $1 billion. We don't even have $1 million ↔ It is still far from being sufficient.\n具体数字概括为不足\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nIt will become popular gradually. 讲的是会逐渐流行，和本题问的 funding（经费够不\n够）根本不是一回事，且录音也没说会流行，属答非所问。\nB) 未提及\n录音说 NASA 只短暂资助、几家机构都不资助，从未提到有定期捐款的 donors，It comes\nregularly from its donors. 里的稳定捐助来源是编造的。\nC) 与原文相反\n录音反复说没钱、NASA 只短暂资助、连 100 万都没有，It has been increased over the\nyears. 说经费逐年增加，与原文的缺钱困境完全相反。\n借用原文：the National Institute of Health won't fund it, and NASA funded it only briefly", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does Nicholas Genovese say about a lot of products we eat today?", "options": ["A) They are not as natural as we believed.", "B) They are less healthy than we expected.", "C) They are more nutritious and delicious.", "D) They are not as expensive as before."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度观点题） They are not as natural as we believed.\n【定位】\"But there are a lot of products that we eat today that are considered natural that are produced\nin a similar manner,\" Genovese said.\n但是我们今天所吃的许多食品，虽被认为是天然的，其实也是用类似（在实验室）的方式生\n产出来的，Genovese 说。\n【信号】转折 But 后才是真意。But there are a lot of products that we eat today that are\nconsidered natural that are produced in a similar manner 以 But 转折开头，前半讲人\n们排斥实验室食物，转折后才抛出 Genovese 的真正观点，答案落在 But 之后。\n【替换】are considered natural that are produced in a similar manner ↔\nThey are not as natural as we believed.\n被视为天然其实并非如此\n【排除】B) 未提及\n录音只谈天不天然、怎么生产，没有比较这些食品是否更健康，They are less healthy\nthan we expected. 的健康话题是录音没有的。\nC) 未提及\n录音在 are considered natural 的语境里谈天然，They are more nutritious and\ndelicious. 却编出更有营养更好吃，原文从未夸这些食品营养美味，是借天然话题臆造的正\n向评价。\nD) 未提及\n录音只字未提价格，They are not as expensive as before. 的贵不贵和原文 Genovese 谈\n的天然与生产方式无关，纯属臆造。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What may add to the cause of stress according to the lecture?", "options": ["A) Exhausting jobs and wasted time.", "B) Wrong eating habits and wasted time.", "C) Pollution and cognitive decline.", "D) Exhausting jobs and wrong eating habits."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Exhausting jobs and wrong eating habits.\n【定位】Our tiring jobs, faulty eating habits and pollution may add to the cause of stress.\n我们累人的工作、不良的饮食习惯以及污染，都可能成为压力的成因。\n【信号】题干原话回响+并列列举。答案句 may add to the cause of stress 与题干 add to the\ncause of stress 几乎同词，这种\"题干措辞在原文回响\"就是答案句信号；紧接其前的三个并\n列名词 Our tiring jobs, faulty eating habits and pollution 就是被问的成因清单。\n【替换】tiring jobs ↔ Exhausting jobs\n同义词替换 tiring=exhausting\nfaulty eating habits ↔ wrong eating habits\n同义词替换 faulty=wrong\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 项 Exhausting jobs and wasted time. 前半 Exhausting jobs 确是录音 tiring jobs 的同\n义改写，听到很想选；但后半 wasted time（浪费时间）录音中根本没出现，属凭一个对的\n要素硬凑。只对上一半即排除。\n借用原文：tiring jobs\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 项 Wrong eating habits and wasted time. 前半 Wrong eating habits 对应录音 faulty\neating habits，是真念到的；后半 wasted time 同样无中生有。一对一错，排除。\n借用原文：faulty eating habits\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Pollution and cognitive decline. 里 Pollution 确属录音点的成因之一；但 cognitive\ndecline（认知衰退）在录音里是压力\"导致的后果\"——an increase in stress hormones\nmight speed up cognitive decline，被本项偷换成压力的\"成因\"，因果颠倒。\n借用原文：speed up cognitive decline", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What can act as a healing therapy to cure personality issues?", "options": ["A) Adequate sleep.", "B) Laugh or smile.", "C) Music.", "D) Exercise."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Music.\n【定位】A combination of psychology and counselling, music can act as a healing therapy to cure\nseveral conditions like depression, personality issues, cancer, etc.\n音乐结合心理学与心理咨询，可以作为一种治愈性疗法，治疗诸如抑郁、人格问题、癌症等\n多种状况。\n【信号】题干短语回响+病症清单含目标词。答案句 music can act as a healing therapy to cure\n与题干 act as a healing therapy to cure 措辞高度重合，是答案句信号；其后病症列举\ndepression, personality issues, cancer 里正含题干要的 personality issues，锁定主语\nmusic 即答案。\n【替换】music ↔ Music.\n主语直取，原词对应\npersonality issues ↔ Music.\n凭目标病症反推手段\n【排除】A) 答非所问\nA 项 Adequate sleep. 对应录音 A good amount of sleep of say 7-8 hours is a must to\nrelax your mind and reduce stress levels，睡眠的功效是放松心神、降压，从未说能治\npersonality issues。听到\"睡眠\"被讲过就选，会落入此项。\n借用原文：reduce stress levels\nB) 答非所问\nB 项 Laugh or smile. 对应录音 try laughing or if not at least smile as it releases\nchemicals which decrease stress level，笑的功效是释放化学物质降低压力，与治疗\npersonality issues 无关。\n借用原文：decrease stress level\nD) 答非所问\nD 项 Exercise. 对应录音 daily physical exercise helps protect your brain from stress-\ninduced depression，运动的功效是保护大脑、防压力性抑郁，没说能治 personality\nissues。\n借用原文：protect your brain", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What can be the reason for a massage lover to be more obsessed with it according to the lecture?", "options": ["A) Massage helps to improve physical function and reduce stress.", "B) Massage plays a role in mood-boosting and is good for sleep.", "C) Essential oils used in massage help to eliminate various diseases.", "D) Essential oils used in massage do good to your memory and thinking."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Massage helps to improve physical function and reduce stress.\n【定位】If you are a massage lover, here is one more reason for you to indulge in it more often as\nmassage improves vascular function and helps in reducing stress.\n如果你是个按摩爱好者，这里再给你一个更常做按摩的理由：因为按摩能改善血管功能，并\n有助于减轻压力。\n【信号】因果 as 引出真正理由。答案句用因果连词 as massage improves vascular function and\nhelps in reducing stress 给出\"更常做按摩\"的理由，as 之后就是落点；而题干 one more\nreason...to be more obsessed 与录音 one more reason for you to indulge in it more\noften 措辞回响，进一步坐实是这一句。\n【替换】improves vascular function ↔ improve physical function\n下位概括成上位 vascular→physical\nhelps in reducing stress ↔ reduce stress\n近原词对应 reducing stress=reduce stress\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\nB 项 Massage plays a role in mood-boosting and is good for sleep. 说按摩提振情绪、\n助眠；但录音讲按摩只说改善血管功能与减压，助眠是睡眠段 reduce stress levels 那类内\n容、提振情绪近似笑/正向思维的功效，被安到了 massage 头上。\n借用原文：improves vascular function and helps in reducing stress\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 项 Essential oils used in massage help to eliminate various diseases. 借了录音真出\n现的 Essential oils help to soothe your mind and eliminate anxiety，但原文 essential\noils 只是\"安抚心神、消除焦虑(anxiety)\"，被本项夸大成\"消除各种疾病(various\ndiseases)\"，范围被放大。\n借用原文：eliminate anxiety\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 项 Essential oils used in massage do good to your memory and thinking. 同样借\nessential oils 做诱饵，但录音说精油是 soothe your mind and eliminate anxiety，从未\n说有益 memory and thinking（记忆与思维），功效被整个换掉。\n借用原文：soothe your mind and eliminate anxiety", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the talk say about political parties before the introduction of television?", "options": ["A) They spent more money in promoting their political candidates.", "B) They attracted more members and focused on candidates' characters.", "C) They had more influence over the selection of political candidates.", "D) They received more money from sponsors as campaign funds."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They had more influence over the selection of political candidates.\n【定位】By giving citizens independent access to the candidates, television diminished the role of the\npolitical party in the selection of the major party candidates.\n通过让公民独立接触候选人，电视削弱了政党在主要政党候选人选拔中的作用。\n【信号】'电视削弱了'反推'之前更强'。答案句说 television diminished the role of the political\nparty in the selection of the major party candidates——电视'削弱'了政党在候选人选拔\n中的作用；既然电视让它'变弱'，反推电视之前政党在这件事上影响力更大。'diminished\n（削弱）'是承载今昔对比的信号词。\n【替换】the role of the political party in the selection of the major party candidates ↔\ninfluence over the selection of political candidates\nrole=influence，同义改写\ntelevision diminished the role ↔ They had more influence\n'削弱'反向推出'过去更多'\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nspent more money in promoting their political candidates 借了 candidates 这个话题\n词诱你选，但录音讲的是政党对候选人'选拔'的影响，从未提政党花钱'推广'候选人，属答非\n所问的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：By giving citizens independent access to the candidates\nB) 张冠李戴\nattracted more members and focused on candidates' characters 里的 character 确实\n是录音念到的词（focus on character rather than issues），但那是讲'电视让公民关注品\n格'，不是讲'政党吸引成员、注重品格'，把别处的词搬来张冠李戴。\n借用原文：television accelerated the citizens' focus on character rather than issues\nD) 未提及\nreceived more money from sponsors as campaign funds 谈竞选资金与赞助商，录音整\n段讲的是政党在候选人选拔中的'影响力'变化，没有一句涉及钱或赞助商，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "Why was traditional political discourse more successful than televised speeches?", "options": ["A) It made politics seem more intimate to citizens.", "B) It provided detailed information about a candidate's behaviour.", "C) It allowed news coverage of political candidates.", "D) It placed political issues within a historical context."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It placed political issues within a historical context.\n【定位】In 15 or 30 seconds, a speaker cannot establish the historical context that shaped the issue in\nquestion, cannot detail the probable causes of the problem, and cannot examine alternative\nproposals to argue that one is preferable to others.\n在15秒或30秒里，演讲者无法建立塑造该议题的历史背景，无法详述问题的可能成因，也无\n法考察各种替代方案以论证某一方案优于其他。\n【信号】电视演讲'cannot'的，正是传统话语能的。这句连用三个 cannot 说电视短演讲做不到什\n么，第一条就是 cannot establish the historical context that shaped the issue（无法建\n立议题的历史背景）；题问'传统话语为何更成功'，答案正是它能做到电视做不到的——置\n入历史背景。'cannot'是反衬信号。\n【替换】cannot establish the historical context that shaped the issue in question ↔\nplaced political issues within a historical context\n电视'不能建历史背景'⇄传统话语'能置入历史背景'\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nmade politics seem more intimate 里的 intimate 是录音第5段形容'电视'的词\n（television is an intimate medium），但'亲密'是电视的属性、不是传统话语更成功的原\n因，既盗原词又把对象弄反。\n借用原文：Because television is an intimate medium\nB) 偷换概念\nprovided detailed information about a candidate's behaviour 看似与原文 detail 一词\n沾边，但录音 cannot detail the probable causes of the problem 讲的是'详述问题成\n因'，根本不是'候选人行为的细节'，偷换了 detail 的对象。\n借用原文：cannot detail the probable causes of the problem\nC) 原词陷阱\nallowed news coverage of political candidates 里的 news coverage 是录音讲电视新闻\n时出现的概念（hear and see a part of the speech on the news），但那是电视带来的现\n象，并非传统话语更成功的理由，借词诱选。\n借用原文：see a part of the speech on the news", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker mean by stating \"politicians assert but do not argue\"?", "options": ["A) Politicians dislike explaining their own positions on specific issues to citizens.", "B) Politicians make claims without providing arguments for the claims.", "C) Politicians enjoy explaining issues and arguing with competitors.", "D) Politicians take stronger positions on political issues than in the past."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） Politicians make claims without providing arguments for the claims.\n【定位】Politicians assert but do not argue.\n政客们只下断言，却不加以论证。\n【信号】题干引号原句即答案句本身。题干直接引用了 Politicians assert but do not argue，这句\n就是要释义的对象；其紧邻上文一串 cannot（无法建立背景、无法详述成因、无法论证优\n劣）正是'do not argue'的注脚，定位明确。\n【替换】Politicians assert ↔ make claims\nassert=make claims\nbut do not argue ↔ without providing arguments for the claims\ndo not argue=without providing arguments\n【排除】A) 过度推断\ndislike explaining their own positions on specific issues 把'不论证'升级成'讨厌解释'，\n录音只说政客'不去论证'（行为），没说他们'不喜欢/讨厌'（情绪态度），属过度推断。\n借用原文：Politicians assert but do not argue\nC) 与原文相反\nenjoy explaining issues and arguing with competitors 说政客喜欢解释和辩论，而原文\n明说他们 do not argue（不论证），极性完全相反，是把答案翻反的反向项。\n借用原文：Politicians assert but do not argue\nD) 未提及\ntake stronger positions on political issues than in the past 谈立场'更强硬'且做今昔对\n比，原句只说'断言不论证'，与立场强弱、与过去比较都无关，凭空生造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "Why do politicians create staged political events?", "options": ["A) They can obtain more television coverage for themselves.", "B) They can create more time to discuss political issues.", "C) They can spend more time talking to citizens in person.", "D) They can engage in debates with their opponents."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （目的题） They can obtain more television coverage for themselves.\n【定位】Recognizing the power of television's pictures, politicians craft televisual, staged events,\ncalled pseudo-events, designed to attract media coverage.\n意识到电视画面的力量，政客们精心制造电视化的、摆拍式的事件，称为'伪事件'，其目的\n是吸引媒体报道。\n【信号】designed to 直接点出目的。答案句末 designed to attract media coverage 用\ndesigned to 明示制造这些事件的目的就是吸引媒体报道；designed to 是目的信号词，听\n到它后面那截就是答案。\n【替换】designed to attract media coverage ↔ obtain more television coverage for themselves\nattract media coverage=obtain television coverage\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\ncreate more time to discuss political issues 里 political issues 是全文反复出现的词，诱\n你联想，但录音通篇强调电视化让政治表达'变短、不论证'，政客做伪事件是为曝光、不是\n为多谈议题，方向相反。\n借用原文：Sound bites in news and answers to questions in debates increasingly sound\nlike advertisements\nC) 未提及\ntalking to citizens in person 谈'当面交流'，而答案句明说政客图的是 media coverage\n（媒体曝光），与'当面'恰好相反，录音也无此意，凭空添加且方向偏。\nD) 原词陷阱\nengage in debates with their opponents 里 debates 是录音末句出现的词（answers to\nquestions in debates），但那是讲'辩论里的回答像广告'，不是政客做伪事件的目的，借\n原词张冠李戴。\n借用原文：answers to questions in debates increasingly sound like advertisements", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What's the main disadvantage of person-on-the-street interviews?", "options": ["A) They are not based on a representative sampling.", "B) They are used only on television broadcasting.", "C) They are not carefully worded and organized.", "D) They reflect political opinions from the public."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They are not based on a representative sampling.\n【定位】While such interviews can be highly entertaining, they are not necessarily an accurate\nindication of public opinion. First, they reflect the opinions of only those people who appear\nat a certain location. ... A survey must be based on a precise, representative sampling if it is to\ngenuinely reflect a broad range of the population.\n尽管这类访谈可以很有娱乐性，它们却未必是民意的准确反映。首先，它们只反映出现在某\n个特定地点的那些人的意见。……一项调查若要真正反映广泛人群，就必须建立在精确的、\n有代表性的抽样之上。\n【信号】让步转折 While… + 否定 not necessarily 后才是真意。这段先用让步 While such\ninterviews can be highly entertaining（虽然很有娱乐性）抬一手，紧接着 they are not\nnecessarily an accurate indication of public opinion 用 not necessarily 打转折否定\n——转折否定之后那句才是录音真正要说的缺点，正是答案句。段末 A survey must be\nbased on a precise, representative sampling 再从正面点出问题根子在抽样不具代表性。\n【替换】not necessarily an accurate indication of public opinion ↔\nnot based on a representative sampling\n缺点的根源被点名：不具代表性的抽样\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到这类随访常见于 local television news shows（地方电视新闻节目），B 项\nused only on television broadcasting 就盗用了 television 这个你耳朵刚听到的词诱你\n选；但录音只说它常出现在电视上，从没说只用于电视广播，更没把这当作缺点，属听到原\n词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：local television news shows\nC) 张冠李戴\nnot carefully worded and organized（措辞与组织不够用心）讲的是问题的措辞问题，那\n是录音第3段 great care in the wording of questions 才谈的另一码事，且是讲整个调查\n要注意的，并非街头随访的缺点。本题问的是 person-on-the-street interviews，毛病在\n抽样不在措辞，C 是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：great care in the wording of questions\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音开头说民调 have become an important part of political life（成了政治生活的重要\n部分），D 项 reflect political opinions from the public 盗了 political 这个词，还把它包\n装成一个优点 / 特征；但这既不是街头随访的缺点，方向也正好与题目问缺点相反，是用原\n词正话反说。\n借用原文：have become an important part of political life", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What is crucial for an effective survey?", "options": ["A) A large number of respondents taking part in the survey.", "B) Carefully worded and understandable questions.", "C) An interviewer's ability to understand respondents' feelings.", "D) A sociologist who is able to interpret the results."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Carefully worded and understandable questions.\n【定位】In preparing to conduct a survey, sociologists must exercise great care in the wording of\nquestions. An effective survey question must be simple and clear enough for people to\nunderstand it. ... Surveys can become indispensable sources of information, but only if the\nsampling is done properly and the questions are worded accurately.\n在准备开展一项调查时，社会学家必须在问题的措辞上格外用心。一个有效的调查问题必须\n简单清晰到让人能看懂。……调查可以成为不可或缺的信息来源，但前提是抽样做得恰当、\n问题措辞准确。\n【信号】强调词 must + only if 锁定关键要素。第3段连用强调词：must exercise great care in\nthe wording of questions 和 An effective survey question must be simple and clear\nenough for people to understand it，两个 must 都把矛头指向问题的措辞要简明易懂；\n段末 only if the sampling is done properly and the questions are worded accurately\n再用 only if 强调措辞准确是必要前提。强调词扎堆处就是答案句。\n【替换】An effective survey question must be simple and clear enough for people to understand it ↔\nCarefully worded and understandable questions\nsimple and clear 对应 carefully worded and understandable\nmust exercise great care in the wording of questions ↔ Carefully worded\ngreat care in the wording = carefully worded\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音确实强调抽样要做好（only if the sampling is done properly），A 项 large number\nof respondents 蹭了这个调查领域的概念，却把关键偷换成受访者数量要多；可录音通篇\n强调的是抽样要有代表性、要做得恰当，从没说样本数量越大越关键，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：only if the sampling is done properly\nC) 张冠李戴\ninterviewer's ability to understand respondents' feelings 取自第4段讲访谈优点的\nprobe for a subject's underlying feelings，那是在比较访谈与问卷各自长处时说的，并非\n有效调查最关键的要素。本题问 crucial for an effective survey，答案在第3段的措辞，C\n是把别段的话搬来张冠李戴。\n借用原文：probe for a subject's underlying feelings\nD) 未提及\n录音开头出现过 sociologists must exercise great care，D 项 sociologist who is able to\ninterpret the results 就盗了 sociologist 这个词；但录音只说社会学家要在措辞上用心，\n从没说会解读结果的社会学家才是有效调查的关键，这层意思是录音没提的，属未提及。\n借用原文：sociologists must exercise great care", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What's the advantage of live interviews over questionnaires?", "options": ["A) Live interviews cost less money and energy.", "B) Live interviews can obtain more information.", "C) Live interviews are easier to carry out.", "D) Live interviews minimize the influence of the researcher."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Live interviews can obtain more information.\n【定位】An interviewer can obtain a high response rate because people find it more difficult to turn\ndown a personal request for an interview than to throw away a written questionnaire. In\naddition, an interviewer can go beyond written questions and probe for a subject's underlying\nfeelings and reasons.\n访谈者能获得较高的回应率，因为比起把一份书面问卷扔掉，人们更难拒绝当面提出的访谈\n请求。此外，访谈者还能超出书面问题之外，探查受访者深层的感受和原因。\n【信号】递进词 In addition 引出访谈独有优势。第4段先讲访谈能拿到 high response rate，再用\n递进词 In addition 引出访谈更深一层的好处——an interviewer can go beyond written\nquestions and probe for a subject's underlying feelings and reasons（能超出书面问\n题、深挖受访者的感受与原因）。这句正是访谈相对问卷独有、且能挖到更多东西的优势，\n是答案句。\n【替换】probe for a subject's underlying feelings and reasons ↔ obtain more information\n深挖感受与原因 = 拿到更多信息\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音末句明说 questionnaires have the advantage of being cheaper and more\nconsistent（更便宜的是问卷），A 项 Live interviews cost less money and energy 把省\n钱这个优点张冠李戴安到了访谈头上，恰好说反了——便宜的是问卷不是访谈，与原文相\n反。\n借用原文：questionnaires have the advantage of being cheaper and more consistent\nC) 未提及\nLive interviews are easier to carry out（访谈更容易开展）听着合理，但录音讲访谈的优\n点只有回应率高和能深挖感受两条，从没说访谈比问卷更好操作；事实上当面访谈通常更费\n事，这是录音未提的想当然，属未提及。\nD) 未提及\nminimize the influence of the researcher（把研究者影响降到最低）措辞很专业、很像学\n术答案，蹭了访谈 / 调查里研究者这一概念；但录音讲访谈优点时压根没提研究者影响大小\n的事，这是录音未提及的内容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) caters\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 a culture 后缺单数第三人称谓语\n【依据】固定搭配 cater to 锁定答案\n空后紧跟介词 to——that __(26)__ to the late-nighter——而 cater to sb. 意为「迎合某\n人需求」，正好对应后文from 24-hour grocery stores to online shopping sites that\nnever close描述的「24 小时服务」。\n【竞争词】N) surpasses 及物动词，不接 to\nsurpass 表「超过」是直接及物，正常说 surpasses sb.／sth.，不和 to 搭配；且「文化超过晚\n睡者」语义不通，与下文 24 小时商店的语境也对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 a culture 后缺单数第三人"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) recommended\n动词\n【词性槽】as 引导的省略状语从句中作过去分词\n【依据】专家「推荐」睡眠时长，搭配最贴\n上文给出具体时长 7 to 9 hours，正是健康建议的标准说法；recommended by sleep\nexperts ＝「如睡眠专家所推荐的」，与「应睡 7–9 小时」逻辑完全吻合。\n【竞争词】D) debated 语义错配：时长不是争议焦点\ndebated 表「被争论」，但 7–9 小时是公认建议而非争议点；后面第二段才出现真正的争议焦\n点（能否补觉），不要把 D 错配到 27 题。\nI) negotiated 搭配不成立：睡眠时长不能被协商\nnegotiate 多用于谈判、协议场景（negotiate a deal／price），不与「睡眠时长」搭配，语义\n荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·as 引导的省略状语从句中作过去分词"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) debated\n形容词\n【词性槽】副词 hotly 后修饰一个形容词作 topic 的定语\n【依据】hotly debated 是高频搭配，意为「激烈争论的」\nhotly debated topic 是英文表达「热议话题」的固定组合；与紧接其后的The latest\nevidence suggests that while it isn't __(29)__, it might help——研究者各执一词地评估\n补觉效果——逻辑一致。\n【竞争词】I) negotiated 搭配不当：话题不能被「协商」\nnegotiated 用于谈判、协议（a negotiated settlement），不与 topic 搭配；hotly\nnegotiated 也不是英文中的常见组合，语义「激烈协商的话题」在科研语境里说不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 I) —— negotiated 词性对、带「协商」色彩，易错配。hotly\ndebated 与 hotly negotiated 在中文里都可勉强译成「激烈协商／争论」，但英语里\n「hotly＋形容词」搭配的形容词高度固定；防坑就靠把 hotly debated／contested／\ndisputed 这一组词作为整块记忆，遇到 hotly＋___ 直接调用。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词 hotly 后修饰一个形容词作 t"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) ideal\n形容词\n【词性槽】while it isn't ___, it might help：be 动词后填表语形容词\n【依据】让步对比定锚：「虽不理想但有帮助」\nwhile it isn't ideal, it might help 是英文中表「让步—有限肯定」的典型模板，正契合上文\nWhether or not we can catch up on sleep—on the weekend, say—is a hotly __(28)__\ntopic——补觉效果有争议，承认「不算理想」但不否认有用。\n【竞争词】L) ready 搭配不成立：it isn't ready 主语指代不通\nready 修饰人或物表「准备好」，主语 it 指「补觉」这种行为，说「它没准备好」逻辑不通，且\n与 might help 的对比关系也对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·while it isn't ___,"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) chronically\n副词\n【词性槽】副词修饰过去分词 sleep-restricted\n【依据】chronically sleep-restricted ＝ 长期睡眠受限\n下文交代受试者要补觉一整个周末，每晚约 10 小时（they logged about 10 hours per\nnight），说明他们平时是「长期」睡眠不足而非偶尔，chronically 正描述这种持续性状\n态；这也是医学文献中「慢性睡眠剥夺」的标准说法。\n【竞争词】A) alternatively 义不通：表「或者」，不能修饰「睡眠受限」\nalternatively 表「作为另一选择／或者」，是逻辑衔接副词，不能用来修饰形容词描述人的状\n态，「或者地睡眠受限」既不合逻辑也不合语法。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·副词修饰过去分词 sleep-restr"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) improvements\n名词\n【词性槽】showed 后缺宾语\n【依据】补觉后胰岛素能力变好＝改善\n上文交代brought __(30)__ sleep-restricted people into the lab for a weekend of sleep\nduring which they logged about 10 hours per night——给长期缺觉者补觉一周末，结\n果当然是身体指标「改善」；下文也补一句catch-up sleep may undo some but not all\nof the damage，与「showed improvements in …」的正向变化语义吻合。\n【竞争词】E) deprivation 语义反向：剥夺与「能力变好」矛盾\ndeprivation 是负面词，「显示出胰岛素能力的剥夺」既不通顺也违背「补觉→好转」的因果\n链。它真正的位置在第 32 题「sleep ___ causes」。\nK) presumption 搭配不成立：presumption 不与 in the ability 搭\npresumption 表「推测」，常见搭配 presumption of innocence、on the presumption that\n…；说「显示出对胰岛素能力的推测」既不通也不合科研语境。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— deprivation 看着像答案，实为反向情感色彩。全文反复出\n现「睡眠不足」主题，deprivation 一看就贴主旨，容易被错配到 31 题；但 31 题they\nshowed __(31)__ in the ability是「补觉后好转」的正向结果，必须用 improvements。防\n坑靠看清「空处描述的是问题还是解决方案」——再切色彩。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·showed 后缺宾语"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) deprivation\n名词\n【词性槽】名词 sleep 与谓语 causes 之间，构成复合名词主语\n【依据】sleep deprivation 是固定术语，指「睡眠剥夺」\n全文核心议题正是「睡得少」造成的伤害，sleep deprivation 是医学／科普文章里描述这\n一现象的标准说法；代入后「睡眠剥夺所造成的伤害」与上文more than half of American\nadults don't get the 7 to 9 hours of shut-eye every night的现象对得严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】K) presumption 搭配不成立：sleep presumption 不是术语\npresumption 是「推测／假设」，与 sleep 不构成合理复合名词；「睡眠推测所造成的伤害」\n语义也荒诞，不合本文医学语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词 sleep 与谓语 causes"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) ready\n形容词\n【词性槽】be＋___＋to do：表语位置缺形容词\n【依据】be ready to do ＝ 愿意／乐意做某事，与 endorse 搭配自然\nisn't ready to endorse 表「还不太愿意支持」，恰好与本段倒数前句「补觉可能有用」的\n有限正面态度形成节制——研究人员谨慎，catch-up sleep may undo some but not all\nof the damage说明效果有限，故 Liu 仍不愿正式背书「少睡后补」的习惯。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be＋___＋to do：表语位置缺形容"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) necessarily\n副词\n【词性槽】are not ___ an effective remedy：副词修饰形容词短语\n【依据】not necessarily ＝ 未必，正合让步语气\n前文while helpful for some先承认安眠药「对部分人有用」，再用 are not necessarily an\neffective remedy 表「也未必是有效解药」——not necessarily 是固定搭配，表「不一\n定」，让步—转折的语气贯通。\n【竞争词】A) alternatively 语义指向反：表「或者」，与让步逻辑不符\nalternatively 表「作为另一选择」，常在句首引出替代方案；这里要表达「未必是」，与「或\n者」完全不是同一逻辑。\nC) chronically 义偏：长期地，不能修饰「是否有效」\nchronically 表「长期地、慢性地」，常修饰疾病或状态（chronically ill），不与「是不是有效\n解药」搭配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·are not ___ an effec"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) alternatively", "B) caters", "C) chronically", "D) debated", "E) deprivation", "F) ideal", "G) improvements", "H) necessarily", "I) negotiated", "J) pierce", "K) presumption", "L) ready", "M) recommended", "N) surpasses", "O) target"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) target\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 will 后接动词原形\n【依据】target one area of the brain ＝ 针对脑部某一区域\n下文there's never going to be a perfect sleeping pill, because you couldn't really\nreplicate(复制) the different chemicals moving in and out of different parts of the\nbrain解释「安眠药不完美」是因为它只能作用于脑部的某一片区域、无法覆盖多片——\ntarget 正表「瞄准／作用于」，与「作用范围有限」的论点完全咬合。\n【竞争词】J) pierce 义偏：刺穿一具体物体，不用于药物作用脑区\npierce 是物理意义的「刺穿」（pierce the skin／ears），不能比喻药物的作用机制；说「安眠\n药刺穿大脑一个区域」既不专业也与下文「作用范围有限」的逻辑对不上。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 will 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "When people find they are powerless to change a situation, they tend to live with it.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】powerless + live with it 因果对\n【定位】This inconsistency largely stems from a feeling of powerlessness. \"When we can't actually\nremove the source of our fear, we tend to adapt psychologically by adopting a range of\ndefense mechanisms,\" says Tom Crompton, change strategist for the environmental\norganization World Wide Fund for Nature.\n这种言行不一主要源于无力感。'当我们无法真正消除恐惧的源头时，我们倾向于通过采取一\n系列防御机制在心理上加以适应，'世界自然基金会的变革策略师 Tom Crompton 说。\n【改写】powerless to change a situation ↔ When we can't actually remove the source of our fear\npowerless ≈ can't actually remove\nthey tend to live with it ↔\nwe tend to adapt psychologically by adopting a range of defense mechanisms\nlive with ↔ adapt psychologically\n【辨邻】 B B 段陈述了民调里 75-80 percent 认为重要却 ranked it last on a list of priorities 的\n现象，但没说'powerless → 只能接受'这一原因机制，与 Q36 的因果配对对不上。\nBut respondents ranked it last on a list of priorities.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "To be effective, environmental messages should be carefully framed.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】environmental messages + carefully framed\n【定位】Cialdini recommends some careful framing of the message. \"Instead of normalising the\nundesirable behaviour, the message needs to marginalise it, for example, by stating that if\neven one person buys yet another SUV, it reduces our ability to be energy-independent.\"\nCialdini 建议要精心包装信息：'信息不该把不良行为常态化，而要把它边缘化，比如可以\n说，哪怕只多一个人再买一辆 SUV，都会削弱我们能源自给自足的能力。'\n【改写】environmental messages should be carefully framed ↔\nCialdini recommends some careful framing of the message\nshould ↔ recommends 建议骨架\nTo be effective ↔\nInstead of normalising the undesirable behaviour, the message needs to marginalise it\n效果意识 ↔ 反命题指导\n【辨邻】 H H 段的核心是 crafting messages that exploit our group mentality 与 what\nmotivates people, what it is that allows them to make change，焦点在'懂得人靠\n什么被驱动'，没出现 framing 这一具体技法。\nBut social scientists are most concerned about crafting messages that exploit our group mentality", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "It is the government's responsibility to persuade people into making environment-friendly decisions.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】government 责任 + persuade 决策\n【定位】They argue that governments should persuade us into making better decisions—such as\nsaving more in our pension plans—by changing the default options.\n他们主张政府应该通过改变默认选项来劝导我们做出更好的决策，比如在养老金计划中存更\n多钱。\n【改写】It is the government's responsibility ↔ They argue that governments should\nresponsibility ↔ should 同义表态\npersuade people into making environment-friendly decisions ↔\npersuade us into making better decisions\npeople↔us、environment-friendly↔better 替换\n【辨邻】 F F 段的 others may have to help us to do so 中 others 是泛指，并未点名\ngovernment；本题题干的责任主体明确是 government，必须排除。\nAnd if we're not going to make rational decisions about the future, others may have to help us to\ndo so.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Politicians are beginning to realise the importance of enlisting psychologists' help in fighting climate change.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Politicians + psychologists' help\n【定位】The power of such simple psychology in fighting climate change is attracting attention across\nthe political establishment.\n这种简单心理学在对抗气候变化中的力量，正在吸引整个政界的关注。\n【改写】Politicians are beginning to realise the importance of ↔\nattracting attention across the political establishment\n视角翻转的同义表达\nenlisting psychologists' help in fighting climate change ↔\nThe power of such simple psychology in fighting climate change\npsychologists' help ↔ simple psychology 名词化对应\n【辨邻】 G G 段是 Thaler 和 Sunstein 等学者主张 governments should persuade us，是规范\n主张；而 Q39 要的是政客正在意识到心理学的重要并开始行动，对应的应是 P 段的现\n实进展。\nThey argue that governments should persuade us into making better decisions", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "To find effective solutions to climate change, it is necessary to understand what motivates people to make change.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】understand what motivates people\n【定位】\"We need to understand what motivates people, what it is that allows them to make change,\"\nsays Professor Neil Adger, of the Tyndall Centre for Climate Change Research in Norwich.\n'我们需要理解是什么驱动着人们、是什么让他们能够做出改变，'Tyndall 气候变化研究中心\n的 Neil Adger 教授说。\n【改写】To find effective solutions to climate change, it is necessary to ↔ We need to understand\n目的-必要 ↔ 直接需要表态\nunderstand what motivates people to make change ↔\nunderstand what motivates people, what it is that allows them to make change\n压缩式同义改写\n【辨邻】 G G 段强调 by changing the default options，属于外部机制层；本题问的是 what\nmotivates people to make change，属于内部驱动层，路径不一致。\ngovernments should persuade us into making better decisions—such as saving more in our pension\nplans—by changing the default options", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "In their evolution, humans have learned to pay attention to the most urgent issues instead of long-term concerns.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】evolution + urgent vs long-term\n【定位】Part of the fault lies with our inner caveman. Evolution has programmed humans to pay most\nattention to issues that will have an immediate impact. \"We worry most about now because if\nwe don't survive for the next minute, we're not going to be around in ten years' time,\" says\nProfessor Elke Weber of the Centre for Research on Environmental Decisions at Columbia\nUniversity in New York.\n部分原因要怪我们体内的穴居人。进化已经把人类编程为最关注那些会产生即时影响的问\n题。'我们最担心当下，因为如果我们撑不过下一分钟，就不会再活到十年后，'哥伦比亚大\n学环境决策研究中心的 Elke Weber 教授说。\n【改写】pay attention to the most urgent issues ↔\npay most attention to issues that will have an immediate impact\nurgent ↔ immediate impact 同义\ninstead of long-term concerns ↔\nour brain discounts the risks—and benefits—associated with issues that lie some way ahead\nlong-term ↔ lie some way ahead\n【辨邻】 E E 段的核心是 Matthew Rushworth 实验室的发现 all agents seem to make\ndecisions is that they assign a lower weighting to outcomes that are going to\nbe further away in the future，没有出现 evolution 这一进化命题，与题干 In their\nevolution 不匹配。\nOne of the ways in which all agents seem to make decisions is that they assign a lower weighting to\noutcomes that are going to be further away in the future", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "One study shows that our neighbours' actions are influential in changing our behaviour.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】neighbours' actions 影响行为\n【定位】But it was the ones that mentioned the actions of neighbours that drove down power use.\n但真正压低用电量的，是那些提到邻居做法的信息。\n【改写】our neighbours' actions ↔ the actions of neighbours\n所有格语序的等值\nOne study shows ↔\nCialdini conducted a study in San Diego in which coat hangers bearing messages about saving\nenergy were hung on people's doors\nOne study ↔ conducted a study 上下位\ninfluential in changing our behaviour ↔ drove down power use\n抽象影响 ↔ 具体行为结果\n【辨邻】 K K 段是用 compare their energy use with the local average，对照的是'本地平均'这\n一统计概念；本题题干钉的是 our neighbours' actions 这种具体邻里行为，对照对象\n不同。\nsimply providing the facility for people to compare their energy use with the local average is\nenough to cause them to modify their behaviour", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Despite clear signs of global warming, it is not easy for most people to believe climate change will affect their own lives.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】global warming 自身不易相信\n【定位】Despite mournful polar bears and charts showing carbon emissions soaring, most people find\nit hard to believe that global warming will affect them personally.\n尽管有忧伤的北极熊和显示碳排放飙升的图表，大多数人仍然觉得很难相信全球变暖会影响\n到自己。\n【改写】Despite clear signs of global warming ↔\nDespite mournful polar bears and charts showing carbon emissions soaring\n上位概括 ↔ 下位具体意象\nit is not easy for most people to believe ↔ most people find it hard to believe\nnot easy ↔ hard 直接同义\naffect their own lives ↔ affect them personally\ntheir own lives ↔ personally 等值\n【辨邻】 D D 段以 Evolution has programmed humans to pay most attention to issues that\nwill have an immediate impact 给出原因机制；本题 Q43 只问'人们不易相信会影响\n自己'这一现象的陈述出处，应回到现象本身的 B 段。\nEvolution has programmed humans to pay most attention to issues that will have an\nimmediate impact", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "We should take our future into consideration in making decisions concerning climate change before it is too late.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】before it is too late + 考虑未来\n【定位】By the time we wake up to the threat posed by climate change, it could well be too late. And\nif we're not going to make rational decisions about the future, others may have to help us to\ndo so.\n等我们意识到气候变化所带来的威胁时，很可能已经为时已晚。如果我们自己不打算就未来\n做出理性决策，那别人就只能来帮我们做。\n【改写】take our future into consideration in making decisions ↔\nmake rational decisions about the future\n考虑未来 ↔ 就未来做决策\nbefore it is too late ↔\nBy the time we wake up to the threat posed by climate change, it could well be too late\nbefore too late ↔ by the time…it could well be too late\n【辨邻】 E E 段只是中性陈述 assign a lower weighting to outcomes that are going to be\nfurther away in the future，没有'我们应该考虑未来、否则太迟'的价值判断，与\nshould 的态度不匹配。\nthey assign a lower weighting to outcomes that are going to be further away in the future", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Existing social networks can be more effective in creating change in people's behaviour.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Existing social networks 更有效\n【定位】\"Using existing civil society structures or networks is a more effective way of creating\nchange… and obviously trade unions are one of the biggest civil society networks in the UK,\"\nhe says.\n'借用既有的公民社会结构或网络，是一种更有效的促成变革的方式……显然，工会是英国最\n大的公民社会网络之一，'他说。\n【改写】Existing social networks ↔ existing civil society structures or networks\nsocial ↔ civil society 上下位\nmore effective in creating change in people's behaviour ↔\na more effective way of creating change\nmore effective + creating change 原样保留\n【辨邻】 M M 段是举例陈述 Erica Gregory. A retired member of the Public and Commercial\nServices Union, she is setting up one of 1,100 action groups，没有给出'更有\n效'这一比较判断，本题题干要的是评价句，应回 O 段。\nTake your average trade union member, chances are they will be politically motivated and be used\nto collective action", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What is traditional educators' interpretation of the research outcome mentioned in the first paragraph?", "options": ["A) Students are not able to apply prior knowledge to new problems.", "B) College students are no better than fifth graders in memorizing issues.", "C) Education has not paid enough attention to major environmental issues.", "D) Education has failed to lead students to think about major scientific ideas."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is traditional educators' interpretation of the research outcome 问的是一\n个明确归属、明确位置的观点判断，不是推断也不是态度\n【拆句】主干是 What is traditional educators' interpretation；of 短语 of the research\noutcome mentioned in the first paragraph 修饰 interpretation；mentioned 是过去分\n词作后置定语修饰 outcome。整句无陷阱，注意 interpretation 在这里不是\"翻译\"而是\"解\n读、解释\"。\n【定位】From the standpoint of a traditional educator, this outcome indicated that schooling had failed\nto help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific ideas.\n从传统教育者的立场看，这一结果表明学校教育未能帮助学生思考生态系统与灭绝这些重大\n科学概念。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\napply prior knowledge to new problems 是研究者自己定义\"未来学习准备\"时提到的\n两种能力之一，且原文 not the ability to retain facts or apply prior knowledge to a\nnew situation 明确说\"区分大学生与小孩的不是这种能力\"，并不是传统教育者从研究结\n果得出的结论\nB) 偷换概念\n原文 the two groups came up with plans of similar quality 是说两组人\"制定\n的方案质量\"相近，没有说他们在\"记忆问题\"上无差异；选项把质量比较偷换成\nmemorizing issues\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说的是 major scientific ideas，举例为 ecosystems and extinction；选项把它窄\n化为 major environmental issues，且把\"未能引导学生思考\"偷换为\"未给予足够关注\"\nD) ✓ 正确\nschooling had failed to help students think about ecosystems and extinction, major scientific\nideas\n↔ Education has failed to lead students to think about major scientific ideas.\nD 项是定位句主干的标准同义替换，无任何添加、无任何偷换，与预判完全一致", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "In what way are college students different from children?", "options": ["A) They have learned to think critically.", "B) They are concerned about social issues.", "C) They are curious about specific features.", "D) They have learned to work independently."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】In what way 后接事实性差异，不是态度也不是推断，文中能找到归属明确的对比\n【拆句】主干是 are college students different from children，in what way 提到句首引导问差\n异；句式平直，无陷阱。\n【定位】The college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical\nthinking. They had learned how to learn.\n大学生已经培养出了提问的能力，这是批判性思维的基石。他们学会了如何学习。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe college students had cultivated the ability to ask questions, the cornerstone of critical\nthinking\n↔ They have learned to think critically.\n预判要的就是\"具备批判性思考能力\"，A 直接以 think critically 命中\nB) 无中生有\nsocial issues 在原文不存在；原文谈的是生态/科学议题（interdependence between\neagles and their habitat），不是社会议题\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 Fifth graders tended to focus on features of individual eagles 明确说\"关注个\n体特征\"的是五年级学生，不是大学生；选项把对象搞反\nD) 无中生有\nwork independently 在原文中没有依据；原文谈的是\"学会如何学习/提问能力\"，与独\n立工作不是一回事", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What is the benefit of asking questions with no ready answers?", "options": ["A) It arouses students' interest in things around them.", "B) It cultivates students' ability to make scientific inquiries.", "C) It trains students' ability to design scientific experiments.", "D) It helps students realize not every question has an answer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is the benefit 问的是一个客观的功能效果，文中应有研究结论的直接陈述\n【拆句】主干 What is the benefit；of 短语 of asking questions with no ready answers 修饰\nbenefit，with no ready answers 进一步修饰 questions（\"没有现成答案的问题\"）。注意\nno ready answers 是双重否定意味的限定，不是\"没有答案\"而是\"答案不是现成的\"。\n【定位】We found that when we taught participants to ask \"What if?\" and \"How can?\" questions that\nnobody present would know the answer to and that would spark exploration, they engaged in\nbetter inquiry at the next exhibit—asking more questions, performing more experiments and\nmaking better interpretations of their results.\n我们发现，当我们教参与者去问那些在场无人知道答案、能激发探索的\"What if?\"和\"How\ncan?\"问题时，他们在下一个展品前进行了更好的科学探究——提更多问题、做更多实验、\n对结果做出更好的解读。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文谈的是探究能力的提升，不是\"激起对周围事物的兴趣\"；arouses students'\ninterest 是常识联想，但原文没有谈\"兴趣\"这层\nB) ✓ 正确\nthey engaged in better inquiry at the next exhibit ↔\nIt cultivates students' ability to make scientific inquiries.\nAsking juicy questions appears to be a transferable skill for deepening collaborative inquiry into\nthe science content\n↔ It cultivates students' ability to make scientific inquiries.\nB 同时被段中因果句和段尾结论句支撑，是最稳的同义改写\nC) 以偏概全\n原文 performing more experiments 只是\"做了更多实验\"，并非\"设计实验的能力\"；选\n项把范围窄化为 design scientific experiments\nD) 过度推断\n原文说的是\"问没有现成答案的问题\"是为了激发探索，从未引导学生去意识到\"不是每个\n问题都有答案\"；not every question has an answer 是过度联想", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is said to be the advantage of informal learning?", "options": ["A) It allows for failures.", "B) It is entertaining.", "C) It charges no tuition.", "D) It meets practical need."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is said to be the advantage 问的是被陈述出来的客观优势，has been said 暗示原\n文有直接陈述\n【拆句】What is said to be the advantage of informal learning：said to be 是被动 + 不定式短\n语作 advantage 的修饰（=据称的优势）。注意不是 \"是 said 的优势\"。\n【定位】Informal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools.\n非正式学习环境比学校更能容忍失败。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nInformal learning environment tolerates failure better than schools ↔ It allows for failures.\nA 把比较句压缩为绝对句但语义核心未变，是最干净的对应\nB) 无中生有\nentertaining 是常识联想——博物馆给人有趣印象，但原文从未把\"娱乐性\"列为非正式\n学习的优势\nC) 偷换概念\n原文末句 gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and\nweekends 提到\"不给分、来者不拒、节假日开放\"，但这是描述系统的开放性，不是题\n干所问的\"优势\"主语句；且原文并未把 no grades 等同于\"免学费\"\nD) 答非所问\n原文 Our society depends on them being able to make critical decisions 谈的\n是\"社会需要人具备这种能力\"，是说为什么必须学会提问，并不是说非正式学习\"满足实\n际需要\"这种优势表述", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author seem to encourage educators to do at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) Train students to think about global issues.", "B) Design more interactive classroom activities.", "C) Make full use of informal learning resources.", "D) Include collaborative inquiry in the curriculum."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author seem to encourage educators to do 用了 seem to，说明\n文中不会有直接的\"教育者应当 X\"原句，要从末段的对比与态度推导出作者意图\n【拆句】主干 What does the author seem to encourage educators to do：seem to do 是\"似乎\n要做某事\"，encourage sb. to do 是\"鼓励某人做\"，两个不定式叠加，注意整体含义是\"作\n者似乎在鼓励教育者去做什么\"，落脚点在\"做什么\"。\n【定位】But people must acquire this skill somewhere. Our society depends on them being able to\nmake critical decisions about their own medical treatment, say, or what we must do about\nglobal energy needs and demands. For that, we have a robust informal system that gives no\ngrades, takes all comers, and is available even on holidays and weekends.\n但人们必须在某处学会这项技能。我们的社会要靠人们具备就医疗决策、能源需求等做出关\n键判断的能力。为此，我们有一套强健的非正式学习系统——不打分、来者不拒、连节假日\n也开放。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nwhat we must do about global energy needs and demands 是\"非正式学习能力派\n得上用场\"的举例，不是作者对教育者的建议；选项把例子误当成建议\nB) 无中生有\ninteractive classroom activities 在原文不存在；作者甚至倾向于\"课堂时间不够\"，没有\n暗示让课堂更互动\nC) ✓ 正确\nwe have a robust informal system that gives no grades, takes all comers, and is available even on\nholidays and weekends\n↔ Make full use of informal learning resources.\nBut people must acquire this skill somewhere ↔\nMake full use of informal learning resources.\n作者把现成系统铺到读者面前，言外之意正是\"用起来\"，C 项是这一步逻辑延伸最准确\n的承接\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是把非正式学习作为课堂之外的兜底途径，没有提倡把它纳入课程；Include\ncollaborative inquiry in the curriculum 反而违背了\"informal learning environment\ntolerates failure better than schools\"的对比立场", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is said about the failure of VSS Enterprise?", "options": ["A) It may lead to the bankruptcy of Virgin Galactic.", "B) It has a strong negative impact on space tourism.", "C) It may discourage rich people from space travel.", "D) It has aroused public attention to safety issues."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is said about the failure of VSS Enterprise? 直接询问关于某具体对象的文中陈述，\n是典型的细节复述结构，没有 infer/imply/main idea 这类标志词。\n【拆句】题干主干 What is said about the failure of VSS Enterprise? 是 \"什么被说\"的被动疑问\n句，of 短语修饰 failure，限定讨论范围。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】On top of the tragic loss of life, the accident in California will cast a long shadow over the\nfuture of space tourism, even before it has properly begun.\n除了悲剧性的生命损失之外，加州的这场事故将会给太空旅游业的未来投下长长的阴影，何\n况这个行业还没有真正起步。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nbankruptcy of Virgin Galactic 文中从未提及破产或财务困境，只谈\"许可证可能被暂\n停\"与\"时间表推迟\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\ncast a long shadow over the future of space tourism ↔\nstrong negative impact on space tourism\n把比喻还原成\"对X产生强烈负面影响\"，与 strong negative impact on space tourism\n字面对应，预判直接命中。\nC) 偷换概念\ndiscourage rich people from space travel 文中虽有 would-be space tourists，但未\n说事故会让富人打消念头，是把行业影响错换为消费者心理。\nD) 偷换概念\naroused public attention to safety issues 文中虽提到监管可能加强，但未说\"引起公\n众对安全问题的关注\"，是把监管动作偷换为公众反应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about the space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic?", "options": ["A) It has just built a craft for commercial flights.", "B) It has sent half a dozen passengers into space.", "C) It was about ready to start regular business.", "D) It is the first to launch \"suborbital\" flights."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic? 仍是对具体对象的客\n观信息复述要求，未要求推断。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about the space-tourism firm Virgin Galactic? 中 the space-\ntourism firm 与 Virgin Galactic 同位语并列，前者是行业类别，后者是公司名，二者互为\n说明。结构直白。\n【定位】Virgin Galactic had, prior to this week's accident, seemed closest to starting regular flights.\n在本周事故发生之前，维珍银河公司似乎是离启动常规商业飞行最近的一家。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\njust built a craft for commercial flights 文中说 a sister craft of the crashed\nspaceplane was reported to be about half-finished —— 姊妹机才造了一半，并\n非\"刚造好\"。\nB) 张冠李戴\nsent half a dozen passengers into space 文中 Just half a dozen holiday-makers\nhave reached orbit since then 指的是整个行业到达过轨道的总人数，非 Virgin\nGalactic 一家所送。\nC) ✓ 正确\nseemed closest to starting regular flights ↔ was about ready to start regular business\n预判\"最接近商业开张\"直接命中 was about ready to start regular business，about\nready 与 closest to 是临近副词的标准同义对应。\nD) 过度推断\nfirst to launch \"suborbital\" flights 文中只说\"多家公司开始规划亚轨道飞行\"，并未声\n明 Virgin Galactic 是首家；且事故发生时还未真正首飞。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What is the purpose of the 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act?", "options": ["A) To ensure space travel safety.", "B) To limit the FAA's functions.", "C) To legalize private space explorations.", "D) To promote the space tourism industry."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What is the purpose of the 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act? 问\n的是该法案被制定时的明确目的，文中通常会有 intended to / aimed at 之类的目的状语\n对应。\n【拆句】题干 What is the purpose of the 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act?\n主干是 What is the purpose of X，of 短语长但内部只是法案全名的堆叠，无嵌套从句。\n【定位】The 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act, intended to encourage private space\nvehicles and services, prohibits the transportation secretary (and thereby the FAA) from\nregulating the design or operation of private spacecraft, unless they have resulted in a serious\nor fatal injury to crew or passengers.\n2004 年的《商业太空发射修正案》旨在鼓励私人太空运载工具和服务的发展，它禁止运输\n部长（以及 FAA）对私人航天器的设计或运行进行监管，除非这些航天器已经导致机组成员\n或乘客严重或致命的伤害。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nensure space travel safety 文中虽提到 safety 相关条款，但安全是该法案的\"例外条\n款\"前提，不是立法初衷；初衷在 intended to 那一句。\nB) 手段当目的（偷换概念）\nlimit the FAA's functions 文中 prohibits the transportation secretary…from\nregulating 确实限制了 FAA，但这是为\"鼓励产业发展\"服务的手段，不是立法目的\n本身。\nC) 无中生有\nlegalize private space explorations 文中从未说该法案使私人航天\"合法化\"——文章预\n设私人航天本就合法，只在监管方式上做安排。\nD) ✓ 正确\nintended to encourage private space vehicles and services ↔\nTo promote the space tourism industry\n预判\"鼓励私人航天\"对应 To promote the space tourism industry，\nencourage→promote 是六级常见同义替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What might the FAA do after the recent accident in California?", "options": ["A) Impose more rigid safety standards.", "B) Stop certifying new space-tourist agencies.", "C) Amend its 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act.", "D) Suspend Virgin Galactic's licence to take passengers into space."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What might the FAA do after the recent accident in California? 表面用 might 略带推\n断色彩，但答案对应文中 could 之类的明文表达，仍是细节复述题。\n【拆句】题干 What might the FAA do after the recent accident in California? 主干 What might\nFAA do，after 短语作时间状语限定时段，结构清晰。\n【定位】That means that the FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly.\n这意味着 FAA 可能暂停维珍银河公司的飞行许可证。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nImpose more rigid safety standards 与文中 insist on checking…as thoroughly as it\ndoes commercial aircraft 略有重合，但题干问 might do，原文最直接的 might 对应\n是 could suspend，而非 insist on checking。\nB) 无中生有\nStop certifying new space-tourist agencies 文中从未提到\"停止认证新机构\"，只谈暂\n停现有公司许可。\nC) 无中生有\nAmend its 2004 Commercial Space Launch Amendments Act 文中只是描述法案现\n行条款，未提任何\"修订\"动作。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe FAA could suspend Virgin Galactic's license to fly ↔\nSuspend Virgin Galactic's licence to take passengers into space\n预判\"吊销飞行许可\"对应 Suspend Virgin Galactic's licence to take passengers into\nspace，suspend 是动词原词复现，命中度最高。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2015-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author think of private space travel?", "options": ["A) It is worth promoting despite the risks involved.", "B) It should not be confined to the rich only.", "C) It should be strictly regulated.", "D) It is too risky to carry on."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of private space travel? 直接索取作者主观判断，需聚合全\n文走向与末段总结性表态。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author think of private space travel? 主干 What does the author\nthink of X，of 短语限定评议对象。结构直白无陷阱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThere is no doubt that spaceflight entails risks ↔ despite the risks involved\nto pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks, and to reduce them with the benefit of\nhard-won experience\n↔ worth promoting\n预判要求\"承认风险 + 仍值得\"两面，It is worth promoting despite the risks\ninvolved 用 despite + worth promoting 一次性覆盖两面，无遗漏。\nB) 以偏概全\nshould not be confined to the rich only 来自 has until now operated largely as\nthe playground of billionaires and dreamy engineers 这一客观现状描述，作者并未\n给出\"应当扩大\"的规范性表态。\nC) 与原文相反\nshould be strictly regulated 文中虽提及 FAA 可能加强检查，但 it would add\nsignificant cost and complexity 显示作者对严管副作用持保留态度，并未主张严管。\nD) 与原文相反\ntoo risky to carry on 与作者 to pioneer a new mode of travel is to free those risks\n的\"开拓性 + 可在经验中降险\"立场直接相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}]});
